-- [Modes of Selection] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A single ancestral cichlid fish colonized a young African lake. Within roughly two million years, researchers document over 300 descendant species inhabiting the same continuous, barrier-free lake basin: some scrape algae from rocks, some crush snail shells, some pick plankton from open water, and some prey on the eggs of other cichlids. Genetic data confirm all 300 species trace back to the one colonizing lineage. Which evolutionary phenomenon does this outcome most specifically illustrate?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Adaptive radiation"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Directional selection"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Allopatric speciation"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Diversifying selection"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' OpenStax defines adaptive radiation as "a rapid ... branching through speciation of a phylogenetic clade into many closely related species," in which the descendants "radiate into different habitats and niches," and cites the Lake Victoria cichlids—which diversified into hundreds of feeding specialists in under three million years—as a spectacular example (lines 63520-63524). The diagnostic signature is therefore many new species descending from one founder lineage, each occupying a distinct niche, arising rapidly. (Choice A) is correct: over 300 species tracing to a single colonizing lineage, each specialized for a different feeding niche and produced by rapid branching speciation, matches every defining feature of adaptive radiation; the many-from-one, niche-filling pattern is what makes this the most specific label. (Choice B) is incorrect: directional selection "selects for phenotypes at one end of the spectrum of existing variation" within a single population, shifting its genetic makeup toward one fit phenotype (lines 23177-23184), as with the peppered moth—it produces no new species and cannot account for 300 descendant lineages filling different niches. (Choice C) is incorrect: allopatric speciation is speciation "via geographic separation," requiring a physical barrier to divide populations; here the 300 species arose within one continuous, barrier-free lake basin (a sympatric setting), so the defining geographic isolation is absent, and even where it applies it names a two-population split rather than a one-to-many diversification. (Choice D) is incorrect: diversifying (disruptive) selection favors two or more extreme phenotypes over intermediates within a single population (lines 23195-23197), such as alpha versus sneaker males of one species—it describes a within-population polymorphism, not the branching of one lineage into hundreds of distinct species across multiple niches. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining criteria of adaptive radiation—one founder lineage, rapid branching into many species, and occupation of multiple distinct niches—to classify a dataset and rule out the geographic-isolation and within-population mechanisms that do not fit the evidence.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'afb0d5bd-b43f-46df-9fcf-5460e12821ae';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'afb0d5bd-b43f-46df-9fcf-5460e12821ae';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('afb0d5bd-b43f-46df-9fcf-5460e12821ae', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing a mode of selection within one population with a multi-species diversification pattern'),
  ('afb0d5bd-b43f-46df-9fcf-5460e12821ae', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'applying a speciation mechanism (geographic isolation) that is a plausible but incorrect fit because the scenario is sympatric'),
  ('afb0d5bd-b43f-46df-9fcf-5460e12821ae', 'D', 'misconception', 'confusing within-population disruptive selection for extremes with the origin of many separate species');

-- [Quantization, Bohr & Spectra] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a hydrogen discharge lamp, excited atoms relax by a variety of downward transitions, each releasing a single photon. The energy of a Bohr level is given by \(E_n = -k/n^2\), where \(k\) is a positive constant. Of the four emission transitions listed below, which one produces the photon with the shortest wavelength?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(n = 4 \\to n = 1\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(n = 6 \\to n = 2\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(n = 3 \\to n = 1\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(n = 2 \\to n = 1\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior.'' The photon emitted in a downward transition carries the energy difference between the two levels, \(\Delta E = E_\text{high} - E_\text{low}\), and by \(E = hc/\lambda\) the shortest-wavelength photon is the one with the largest \(|\Delta E|\). Because \(E_n = -k/n^2\), the levels are packed together near the top and spread far apart near the bottom, so the size of a gap depends far more on how low the transition lands than on how many levels it spans. Evaluating \(-k/n^2\): \(E_1 = -k\), \(E_2 = -k/4\), \(E_3 = -k/9\), \(E_4 = -k/16\), \(E_6 = -k/36\). (Choice A) is correct: \(n = 4 \to n = 1\) spans from \(-k/16\) down to \(-k\), a gap of \(0.9375k\) — the largest of the four because it ends in the deep \(n = 1\) level while starting high enough to add most of the remaining span, giving the highest-energy, shortest-wavelength photon. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(n = 6 \to n = 2\) crosses the most levels (four steps), which tempts students who equate a bigger jump in \(n\) with more energy, but it runs from \(-k/36\) only down to \(-k/4\), a gap of just \(0.222k\) — the smallest here and the longest wavelength — because the upper levels are crowded and it never reaches \(n = 1\). (Choice C) is incorrect: \(n = 3 \to n = 1\) also lands in the ground level and is close, but its gap is \(k - k/9 = 0.889k\), slightly less than \(n = 4 \to n = 1\); starting one level lower than \(n = 4\) leaves out a small but decisive slice of energy. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(n = 2 \to n = 1\) ends in the ground level too, yet with a gap of only \(k - k/4 = 0.750k\) it is the smallest of the three transitions terminating at \(n = 1\), so its photon is not the shortest wavelength. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the \(E_n = -k/n^2\) energy-level formula to rank transition gaps and connect the largest gap to the shortest-wavelength photon, rather than recognize a stated fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '02035f8c-0da5-4b7c-905c-96564d538805';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '02035f8c-0da5-4b7c-905c-96564d538805';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('02035f8c-0da5-4b7c-905c-96564d538805', 'B', 'misconception', 'larger n-jump means more energy'),
  ('02035f8c-0da5-4b7c-905c-96564d538805', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct reasoning, off-by-one on starting level'),
  ('02035f8c-0da5-4b7c-905c-96564d538805', 'D', 'misconception', 'lands on ground state so assumed maximal');

-- [Prokaryotic Growth & Metabolism] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A microbiologist deposits a single Escherichia coli cell onto sterile nutrient agar. Eighteen hours later she observes a colony of roughly 10^8 cells, all genetically identical to the founding cell. When she replica-plates the colony onto ampicillin, only a handful of rare cells survive. Which statement best accounts for BOTH the overall genetic uniformity of the colony AND the appearance of these rare resistant survivors?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The colony expanded through an asexual division that simply duplicates the genome and hands one complete copy to each of two identical offspring, so the process itself introduces no new variation; the rare resistant cells must instead have arisen from mutation or newly acquired DNA."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cells proliferated by mitosis, and crossing over between homologous chromosomes during each division reshuffled alleles, occasionally generating the resistant survivors."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Each division randomly assorts the parent cell''s two chromosome copies into the daughters, so sibling cells routinely differ, and the resistant cells are merely one of these ordinary variants."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The founder reproduced by transferring plasmid DNA through a pilus into each new cell, and the resistance plasmid it passed down is what made the surviving cells resistant."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes reproduce asexually by binary fission: the single circular chromosome is replicated and the enlarged cell is "pinched inward at its equator and the two resulting cells, which are clones, separate," a process that "does not provide an opportunity for genetic recombination or genetic diversity" (openstax-biology-2e.txt, lines 26310-26315). Because every division yields clones, a colony grown from one founder is genetically uniform, and any new trait such as ampicillin resistance in a few rare cells must originate outside the division process itself — from a spontaneous mutation or from DNA acquired by transformation, transduction, or conjugation. (Choice A) is correct: it correctly frames the division as a clonal, variation-free copying event and correctly attributes the rare resistant survivors to a separate source (mutation or acquired DNA), matching both observations. (Choice B) is incorrect: prokaryotes do not divide by mitosis, and there is no crossing over between homologous chromosomes because a bacterium carries a single chromosome, not homologous pairs, so this cannot generate the survivors. (Choice C) is incorrect: fission produces genetically identical daughters, not routinely differing ones; a parent bacterium does not carry "two chromosome copies" to assort in the way described, and treating the resistant cells as ordinary variants contradicts the fact that they are rare within a uniform colony. (Choice D) is incorrect: conjugation transfers DNA between cells through a pilus but it is horizontal gene transfer, not the division that builds the colony; if a resistance plasmid had been handed to every daughter, the whole colony would be resistant rather than only a rare few. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that binary fission is clonal — producing no genetic variation — to conclude that the colony''s uniformity comes from the division while the rare resistant cells must come from an independent source such as mutation or acquired DNA.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '690f625a-2025-4f33-aa5f-cb6dd3290bbc';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '690f625a-2025-4f33-aa5f-cb6dd3290bbc';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('690f625a-2025-4f33-aa5f-cb6dd3290bbc', 'B', 'misconception', 'applies eukaryotic mitosis/crossing over to prokaryotes'),
  ('690f625a-2025-4f33-aa5f-cb6dd3290bbc', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes fission generates diversity via chromosome assortment'),
  ('690f625a-2025-4f33-aa5f-cb6dd3290bbc', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses conjugation (horizontal gene transfer) with reproductive division');

-- [Electron Configurations] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A simple filling model predicts that neutral chromium (\(Z=24\)) should have the ground-state configuration \([\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^4\). Experimentally, however, the measured ground state of chromium differs from this prediction. Which of the following is the actual ground-state electron configuration of a neutral chromium atom?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\([\\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^3 4p^1\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\([\\text{Ar}]4s^1 3d^5\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\([\\text{Ar}]3d^6\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\([\\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^4\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior.'' The naive Aufbau order predicts \([\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^4\) for chromium, but the \(4s\) and \(3d\) subshells lie very close in energy, and a completely half-filled \(3d\) subshell (\(3d^5\), one electron in each of the five degenerate \(d\) orbitals) represents a condition of extra stability. To reach it, one \(4s\) electron shifts into \(3d\), giving the observed ground state \([\text{Ar}]4s^1 3d^5\). Every option below still contains 24 electrons, so counting alone cannot decide the answer. (Choice B) is correct: moving a single \(4s\) electron into \(3d\) produces the half-filled \(3d^5\) configuration \([\text{Ar}]4s^1 3d^5\), the extra-stable arrangement that is actually observed. (Choice A) is incorrect: promoting a \(3d\) electron up to the higher-energy \(4p\) subshell (\([\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^3 4p^1\)) raises the atom''s energy rather than lowering it, so it is an excited state, not the ground state. (Choice C) is incorrect: emptying the \(4s\) subshell entirely to make \([\text{Ar}]3d^6\) over-applies the shift; only one \(4s\) electron relocates, because removing both costs more energy than the modest stabilization gained. (Choice D) is incorrect: \([\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^4\) is exactly the simple prediction the stem tells you is NOT observed, since it misses the half-filled-\(d\) stabilization. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that half-filled and filled \(d\) subshells confer extra stability to select the correct anomalous ground-state configuration rather than reading off a filling order.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '7ae95ab2-a6b8-4fb6-a866-43cf93903ba1';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7ae95ab2-a6b8-4fb6-a866-43cf93903ba1';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7ae95ab2-a6b8-4fb6-a866-43cf93903ba1', 'A', 'misconception', 'promotes an electron to a higher subshell (4p) instead of rearranging within the near-degenerate 4s/3d set'),
  ('7ae95ab2-a6b8-4fb6-a866-43cf93903ba1', 'C', 'misconception', 'over-applies the 4s-to-3d shift by emptying 4s entirely to maximize d-occupancy'),
  ('7ae95ab2-a6b8-4fb6-a866-43cf93903ba1', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'the correct naive-Aufbau prediction, which the stem explicitly states is not the observed ground state');

-- [Types of Elements] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A newly characterized solid element, X, is brittle and shatters rather than bending when struck, conducts electricity far better than solid \(\text{S}\) but far more poorly than \(\text{Cu}\), and is used as the active material in the semiconductor chips of electronic devices. Based on this profile of physical properties, element X is best classified as',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "a metal, such as \\(\\text{Al}\\) or \\(\\text{Fe}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "a nonmetal, such as \\(\\text{Cl}\\) or \\(\\text{P}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "a metalloid, such as \\(\\text{Si}\\) or \\(\\text{Ge}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "a transition metal, such as \\(\\text{Ni}\\) or \\(\\text{Zn}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.'' The periodic table sorts elements into three broad classes by their bundle of physical properties: metals are shiny, malleable (deform without breaking), and good conductors of heat and electricity; nonmetals are dull, brittle, and poor conductors; and metalloids fall in between, conducting only moderately well and behaving as semiconductors. To classify element X you must match its full property profile against these classes rather than any single clue. (Choice C) is correct: element X is brittle (a nonmetal-like trait), yet conducts electricity moderately—better than the nonmetal \(\text{S}\) but far worse than the metal \(\text{Cu}\)—and serves as a semiconductor, which is precisely the intermediate metal/nonmetal behavior that defines a metalloid such as \(\text{Si}\) or \(\text{Ge}\). (Choice A) is incorrect: a metal would be malleable rather than brittle and would conduct electricity nearly as well as \(\text{Cu}\), not far more poorly, so element X fails the defining conductivity and mechanical tests for a metal. (Choice B) is incorrect: element X is brittle like a nonmetal, but a true nonmetal is a poor conductor comparable to \(\text{S}\); because X conducts substantially better than \(\text{S}\) and functions as a semiconductor, it lies above the nonmetal class in conductivity. (Choice D) is incorrect: transition metals are a subset of the metals and share metallic malleability and high conductivity, so a brittle, only-moderately-conducting semiconductor cannot belong to this group. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the property criteria that distinguish metals, nonmetals, and metalloids to an unknown element''s measured behavior rather than recall a single definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '70281532-f3a1-417a-b314-0803bc553d5c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '70281532-f3a1-417a-b314-0803bc553d5c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('70281532-f3a1-417a-b314-0803bc553d5c', 'A', 'misconception', 'treats any element that conducts electricity as a metal, ignoring brittleness and the intermediate conductivity'),
  ('70281532-f3a1-417a-b314-0803bc553d5c', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'fixates on brittleness as a nonmetal trait while overlooking the semiconducting, moderate-conductivity behavior'),
  ('70281532-f3a1-417a-b314-0803bc553d5c', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'picks a real metal subclass because ''used in electronic devices'' sounds metallic, ignoring that transition metals are malleable good conductors');

-- [Prokaryotic Genetics: Transformation] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher lyses a culture of antibiotic-resistant bacteria and passes the lysate through a preparation step, then adds it to a growing culture of antibiotic-sensitive cells of the same species. After incubation, a small fraction of the previously sensitive cells now grow on antibiotic plates. To identify the mechanism, the researcher repeats the experiment but treats the donor lysate with DNase (an enzyme that degrades unprotected DNA) before adding it to the recipients; in this repeat, NO resistant colonies appear. Which conclusion is best supported by the DNase result?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Conjugation is responsible, because the resistance plasmid must have been pumped through a pilus that the DNase enzyme blocked from forming"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Transduction is responsible, because the DNase degraded the bacteriophage capsids that were carrying the resistance gene into the recipients"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Transformation is responsible, because the transferred genetic material was exposed as free DNA in the medium, where DNase could destroy it before uptake"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Spontaneous mutation is responsible, because the DNase eliminated the mutagen in the lysate that had been inducing resistance alleles in the recipients"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes exchange genes by three horizontal-transfer routes that differ in how the DNA travels: in transformation "the cell takes up prokaryotic DNA directly from the environment," in transduction "a bacteriophage injects DNA" it carries inside a protein capsid, and in conjugation "DNA is transferred from one cell to another via a mating bridge, or pilus" (openstax-biology-2e.txt, lines ~26334-26338). The key to this experiment is that DNase can only degrade DNA that is unprotected and exposed in the medium, so the mechanism must be one in which the transferred DNA is naked when the enzyme acts. (Choice A) is incorrect: conjugation moves DNA through direct cell-to-cell contact via a pilus, so the DNA is never exposed to the surrounding medium and would be shielded from DNase; moreover, a cell-free lysate contains no living donor to extend a pilus, and DNase does not block pilus formation. (Choice B) is incorrect: in transduction the resistance gene rides inside a bacteriophage capsid, which is made of protein and protects the packaged DNA from DNase—DNase does not degrade protein capsids, so transduction would survive this treatment rather than be abolished by it. (Choice C) is correct: because degrading exposed DNA with DNase eliminated the transfer, the resistance-conferring material must have been free DNA in the medium available for uptake by competent recipients, which is the defining feature of transformation—the recipient "takes up prokaryotic DNA directly from the environment" (line ~26335) and recombines it. (Choice D) is incorrect: if resistance had arisen by spontaneous mutation within the recipients, it would be independent of any donor DNA; destroying DNA in the lysate with DNase would have no reason to prevent the recipients'' own mutations, so the loss of resistant colonies rules this out. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the physical properties of each transfer mechanism—whether the DNA is naked in the medium, sealed in a capsid, or passed cell-to-cell—to predict which one is uniquely abolished by degrading exposed DNA.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '4e6404a7-8b92-4da9-8cbf-e69c2c4d2750';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4e6404a7-8b92-4da9-8cbf-e69c2c4d2750';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4e6404a7-8b92-4da9-8cbf-e69c2c4d2750', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses the DNase target with a mechanism that shields DNA from the medium'),
  ('4e6404a7-8b92-4da9-8cbf-e69c2c4d2750', 'B', 'misconception', 'believes DNase degrades protein phage capsids and thus abolishes transduction'),
  ('4e6404a7-8b92-4da9-8cbf-e69c2c4d2750', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'treats externally added DNA as an internal mutagen, inverting the source of the new allele');

-- [Tissues: Connective Tissue] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A pathologist examines a micrograph of a tendon and notes relatively few resident cells scattered among wide, parallel bundles of collagen fibers set in ground substance. To confirm the specimen is connective tissue and not another tissue type, the pathologist next identifies the cell type responsible for synthesizing the collagen and other matrix components seen in this field. Which cell should the pathologist expect to find?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A fibroblast, which secretes the collagen fibers and matrix of most connective tissues"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A chondrocyte, which sits in a lacuna and produces the matrix of cartilage"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An osteoblast, which deposits the mineralized collagen matrix of bone"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A squamous epithelial cell, which is anchored to neighbors by desmosomes and rests on a basement membrane"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Connective tissue consists of relatively few cells dispersed in an abundant extracellular matrix, and the principal cell that manufactures that matrix is the fibroblast; the text states that "the principal cell of connective tissues is the fibroblast. This cell makes the fibers found in nearly all of the connective tissues," and that the collagen and other components of the connective tissue matrix "are secreted by fibroblasts." A tendon is dense regular (fibrous) connective tissue containing "large amounts of collagen fibers and few cells," so the cell producing the collagen seen in this field is the fibroblast. (Choice A) is correct: the fibroblast is the resident cell of tendon and other fibrous connective tissues, and it synthesizes the collagen fibers and ground substance that dominate the micrograph. (Choice B) is incorrect: chondrocytes produce matrix, but they do so specifically in cartilage, where they sit in cavities called lacunae; a tendon is not cartilage, so its collagen is laid down by fibroblasts, not chondrocytes. (Choice C) is incorrect: osteoblasts deposit the mineralized collagen matrix of bone tissue, not the unmineralized parallel collagen bundles of a tendon; the presence of ground substance without mineralization and the tendon architecture point to fibroblasts. (Choice D) is incorrect: squamous epithelial cells are packed tightly, joined by desmosomes, and rest on a basement membrane with little extracellular matrix, so they neither describe the tissue shown nor produce the abundant collagen matrix seen here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of which resident cell builds the matrix of each connective tissue subtype to a tendon micrograph, rather than recognizing a restated definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '94322d02-f090-4322-b4c6-7e54ad6d1bb9';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '94322d02-f090-4322-b4c6-7e54ad6d1bb9';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('94322d02-f090-4322-b4c6-7e54ad6d1bb9', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'right-concept-wrong-tissue'),
  ('94322d02-f090-4322-b4c6-7e54ad6d1bb9', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'right-concept-wrong-tissue'),
  ('94322d02-f090-4322-b4c6-7e54ad6d1bb9', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses-epithelial-with-connective');

-- [Types of Reactions] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student is asked to sort four balanced reactions by type. Each equation below is correctly balanced, but only one belongs to the class that produces a salt and water from an acid and a base. Which reaction is a neutralization reaction?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{CH}_4(g) + 2\\,\\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CO}_2(g) + 2\\,\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g)\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{HBr}(aq) + \\text{KOH}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{KBr}(aq) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Zn}(s) + 2\\,\\text{HCl}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{ZnCl}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(2\\,\\text{Na}(s) + \\text{Cl}_2(g) \\rightarrow 2\\,\\text{NaCl}(s)\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' A neutralization reaction is a specific type of acid-base reaction in which the reactants are an acid and a base (but not water), and the products are a salt and water; an \(\text{H}^+\) from the acid combines with an \(\text{OH}^-\) from the base to form \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), while the remaining ions pair as the salt. Correctly classifying a reaction requires inspecting the actual reactants and products, not just noticing that water or a salt appears somewhere in the equation. (Choice B) is correct: the reactants are the acid \(\text{HBr}\) and the base \(\text{KOH}\), and the products are the salt \(\text{KBr}\) plus \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\)—exactly the acid-plus-base-gives-salt-plus-water signature of neutralization, and neither reactant is water. (Choice A) is incorrect: this is a combustion reaction—a fuel \((\text{CH}_4)\) reacts with molecular oxygen \((\text{O}_2)\) to release energy and produce \(\text{CO}_2\) and water. Water is a product, but there is no acid or base and no salt, so it is not neutralization. (Choice C) is incorrect: the free element \(\text{Zn}\) is oxidized and displaces hydrogen from \(\text{HCl}\), making this a single-displacement (redox) reaction. It is tempting because an acid \((\text{HCl})\) reacts and a dissolved salt \((\text{ZnCl}_2)\) forms, but the second reactant is a metal rather than a base, and \(\text{H}_2\) gas—not water—is produced, so it is not a neutralization. (Choice D) is incorrect: two reactants combine into a single product, \(\text{NaCl}\), which is a combination (synthesis) reaction. A salt is formed, but from an element and a diatomic gas rather than from an acid and a base, and no water is produced. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of a neutralization reaction to classify unfamiliar balanced equations, distinguishing it from combustion, single-displacement, and combination reactions that also involve a salt or water.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b2696998-2b14-46f5-91d3-52cad9b00ef1';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b2696998-2b14-46f5-91d3-52cad9b00ef1';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b2696998-2b14-46f5-91d3-52cad9b00ef1', 'A', 'misconception', 'water-product-means-neutralization'),
  ('b2696998-2b14-46f5-91d3-52cad9b00ef1', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'acid-plus-salt-looks-like-neutralization'),
