-- [Electrolysis & Faraday's Law] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two electrolytic cells are wired in series so that the same quantity of electric charge passes through both. The first cell electroplates silver from a silver nitrate solution, and the second cell deposits aluminum metal from a molten aluminum salt. When the current is run long enough to deposit exactly \(1.0\) mole of silver in the first cell, how many moles of aluminum are deposited in the second cell?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(0.33\\) mole of aluminum"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(1.0\\) mole of aluminum"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(0.50\\) mole of aluminum"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(3.0\\) moles of aluminum"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Electrochemistry (GC).'' By Faraday''s law, the moles of metal deposited at a cathode equal the moles of electrons transferred divided by the charge \(n\) on the metal ion, since each ion must gain \(n\) electrons to be reduced. Because the cells are in series, the same number of moles of electrons flows through both, so the metal requiring more electrons per ion is deposited in a smaller molar amount. (Choice A) is correct: silver deposits as \(\text{Ag}^+ + e^- \rightarrow \text{Ag}\), so \(1.0\) mole of silver consumes \(1.0\) mole of electrons; that same \(1.0\) mole of electrons must reduce \(\text{Al}^{3+}\), which needs \(3\) electrons per ion, yielding \(1.0/3 \approx 0.33\) mole of aluminum. (Choice B) is incorrect: it assumes aluminum and silver require the same number of electrons per ion, which would only be true if aluminum ions carried a \(1+\) charge; \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) is triply charged, so equal charge cannot deposit equal moles. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(0.50\) mole would result if aluminum ions were \(\text{Al}^{2+}\) (dividing \(1.0\) mole of electrons by \(2\)), but aluminum''s stable ion is \(3+\), not \(2+\). (Choice D) is incorrect: it reverses the relationship by multiplying by \(3\) instead of dividing; more electrons per ion means fewer, not more, moles of aluminum are produced from the same charge. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply Faraday''s law to relate the fixed amount of charge to the differing electron requirements of \(\text{Ag}^+\) and \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) and compute the resulting molar amount.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'acbbf347-d56b-442d-a6ec-aff9207f789c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'acbbf347-d56b-442d-a6ec-aff9207f789c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('acbbf347-d56b-442d-a6ec-aff9207f789c', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes all metal ions require one electron per ion (ignores ionic charge)'),
  ('acbbf347-d56b-442d-a6ec-aff9207f789c', 'C', 'misconception', 'uses wrong ionic charge (treats aluminum as 2+ instead of 3+)'),
  ('acbbf347-d56b-442d-a6ec-aff9207f789c', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'multiplies by n instead of dividing, inverting the moles-vs-charge relationship');

-- [Strong vs Weak Acids/Bases; Ka and Kb] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student prepares four separate 0.10 M aqueous solutions, one of each base below, and measures the pH of each at 25 °C:

\(\text{NH}_3\): pH = 11.1
\(\text{CsOH}\): pH = 13.0
\(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_2\): pH = 11.8
\(\text{C}_5\text{H}_5\text{N}\) (pyridine): pH = 9.1

For exactly one of these solutions the measured \([\text{OH}^-]\) turns out to equal the full 0.10 M formal concentration of the base — the signature of a base that has ionized completely. Using the measured pH values, for which base is this the case?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{NH}_3\\), whose pH of 11.1 is clearly basic and reflects the ammonia that has reacted with water"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{CsOH}\\): its pH of 13.0 gives \\(\\text{pOH}=1.0\\), so \\([\\text{OH}^-]=10^{-1.0}=0.10\\ \\text{M}\\) — matching the formal concentration"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{MH}_2\\): its pH of 11.8 is the second-highest, so its \\([\\text{OH}^-]\\) has essentially reached the 0.10 M formal concentration"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_5\\text{N}\\), because pyridine''s aromatic nitrogen donates its lone pair fully to accept a proton"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).'' A base that ionizes completely converts its entire formal concentration into hydroxide, so a 0.10 M solution would give \([\text{OH}^-]=0.10\ \text{M}\), \(\text{pOH}=1.0\), and \(\text{pH}=13.0\); a base that ionizes only partially (finite \(K_b\)) reaches a much lower \([\text{OH}^-]\) and therefore a pH well below 13.0. The task is to convert each measured pH to \([\text{OH}^-]\) and find the one solution whose hydroxide output equals its formal concentration, not to recognize a stated definition. (Choice B) is correct: a pH of 13.0 gives \(\text{pOH}=14.0-13.0=1.0\) and \([\text{OH}^-]=10^{-1.0}=0.10\ \text{M}\), exactly the 0.10 M formal concentration — the signature of complete ionization; \(\text{CsOH}\), a soluble ionic hydroxide, dissociates fully as \(\text{CsOH}\rightarrow\text{Cs}^+ + \text{OH}^-\). (Choice A) is incorrect: pH 11.1 gives \(\text{pOH}=2.9\) and \([\text{OH}^-]\approx1.3\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}\), far below 0.10 M, so \(\text{NH}_3\) has ionized only slightly (small \(K_b\)) — it is basic but nowhere near complete. (Choice C) is incorrect: pH 11.8 gives \([\text{OH}^-]\approx6\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}\), still more than an order of magnitude short of 0.10 M; \(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_2\) ionizes more than ammonia (larger \(K_b\)), which is why its pH is second-highest, but a larger weak-base \(K_b\) does not mean it reaches its formal concentration — it remains only partially ionized. (Choice D) is incorrect: pyridine''s pH of 9.1 corresponds to \([\text{OH}^-]\approx1.3\times10^{-5}\ \text{M}\), the smallest of the set; its aromatic lone pair is weakly available (very small \(K_b\)), so it is the least ionized, not fully ionized. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship \(\text{pH}\rightarrow\text{pOH}\rightarrow[\text{OH}^-]\) to each solution and infer which base''s hydroxide output equals its formal concentration, the defining behavior of complete ionization.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '26e1d802-8b95-46ad-a933-7f2d92c54227';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '26e1d802-8b95-46ad-a933-7f2d92c54227';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('26e1d802-8b95-46ad-a933-7f2d92c54227', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses ''basic solution'' with ''complete ionization'''),
  ('26e1d802-8b95-46ad-a933-7f2d92c54227', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes largest weak-base Kb (highest pH among weak bases) means the base reaches its formal concentration'),
  ('26e1d802-8b95-46ad-a933-7f2d92c54227', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes any nitrogen lone pair implies full proton acceptance');

-- [The Synapse & Synaptic Terminal] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a cultured neuron, researchers apply a drug that selectively blocks voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels at a single site on the cell. After the drug is applied, action potentials still propagate normally along the axon, but no neurotransmitter is released onto the target cell. At which structure must these Ca²⁺ channels have been located for the block to produce this specific effect?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Node of Ranvier"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Axon hillock"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Synaptic end bulb"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Dendritic spine"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' At the synapse, the presynaptic terminal of the axon is the site where depolarization opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels; the resulting Ca²⁺ influx triggers synaptic vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter release. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that the presynaptic element is the synaptic end bulb of the axon, "where Ca²⁺ enters the bulb to cause vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter release," and that when the action potential travels from the axon hillock to the axon terminals and into the end bulbs, "it causes the release of a signaling molecule called a neurotransmitter." The scenario preserves action potential propagation but abolishes release, so the blocked Ca²⁺ channels must sit at the presynaptic terminal itself, not at a structure involved in conducting or initiating the impulse. (Choice A) is incorrect: a node of Ranvier is a gap in the myelin along the axon where the action potential regenerates during saltatory conduction; blocking Ca²⁺ channels there would tend to disrupt propagation, but the scenario states propagation is intact, and nodes are not the site of vesicle fusion. (Choice B) is incorrect: the axon hillock is where the cell body gives rise to the axon and where the action potential is initiated; a block here would affect whether the impulse starts, not release at a normally-conducting terminal. (Choice C) is correct: the synaptic end bulb is the presynaptic terminal where Ca²⁺ entry drives vesicle fusion, so a Ca²⁺-channel block confined to this site abolishes neurotransmitter release while leaving upstream axonal conduction unaffected. (Choice D) is incorrect: the dendritic spine is a postsynaptic protrusion that receives input on the target cell; it is not part of the presynaptic neuron''s release machinery, so blocking channels there would not explain a failure of this neuron to release transmitter. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the role of presynaptic Ca²⁺ influx in vesicle fusion to localize the block from its functional consequences, rather than recall a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '4de91c60-c2d1-4b7a-9efc-b616cc8054c6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4de91c60-c2d1-4b7a-9efc-b616cc8054c6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4de91c60-c2d1-4b7a-9efc-b616cc8054c6', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses conduction site with release site'),
  ('4de91c60-c2d1-4b7a-9efc-b616cc8054c6', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses impulse-initiation site with release site'),
  ('4de91c60-c2d1-4b7a-9efc-b616cc8054c6', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses postsynaptic receiving structure with presynaptic terminal');

-- [Cell Theory & Prokaryotic Fundamentals] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two Escherichia coli strains are mixed in culture. The donor strain carries an antibiotic-resistance gene, and after co-incubation the previously susceptible recipient strain becomes resistant and passes the trait to its own descendants. Sequencing shows the recipient''s original single circular chromosome is unchanged in length, and the newly acquired resistance gene sits on a separate small circular DNA loop. In the recipient cell, the structure that continues to house all of the cell''s essential, always-inherited genes is best identified as the:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Nucleoid"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Plasmid"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Nuclear envelope"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Bacterial capsule"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' In a prokaryote, the single circular chromosome that carries the cell''s essential, reliably inherited genes is concentrated in the nucleoid, whereas non-essential, often transferable genes (such as antibiotic resistance) frequently ride on separate extrachromosomal loops. OpenStax states that "the central region of the cell in which prokaryotic DNA resides is called the nucleoid region," while "prokaryotes often have abundant plasmids, which are shorter, circular DNA molecules" that "can be transferred independently of the bacterial chromosome during cell division and often carry traits such as those involved with antibiotic resistance" (lines 18040-18043). The clue that the original chromosome is unchanged and the new gene sits on a separate loop tells you the resistance came from a plasmid, so the always-inherited essential genes must still be in the chromosome-holding region. (Choice A) is correct: the nucleoid is the region holding the intact single circular chromosome, which carries the essential genes present in every descendant regardless of the plasmid exchange. (Choice B) is incorrect: the plasmid is the separate small loop that carried the resistance gene into the cell; it is extrachromosomal and non-essential, and here it is what was gained, not the structure housing the essential genome. (Choice C) is incorrect: a nuclear envelope is the double membrane that encloses a eukaryotic nucleus, and prokaryotes lack this membrane-bound structure entirely, so no bacterial DNA is housed there. (Choice D) is incorrect: the capsule is an external polysaccharide layer outside the cell wall used for attachment and protection and contains no DNA at all. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the chromosomal nucleoid and transferable extrachromosomal plasmids to a conjugation experiment, using the data (unchanged chromosome length, separate resistance loop) to identify where the essential genome resides.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a7b7c23f-b288-4f27-9540-1b9061838723';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a7b7c23f-b288-4f27-9540-1b9061838723';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a7b7c23f-b288-4f27-9540-1b9061838723', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses the transferred extrachromosomal loop (plasmid) with the structure housing the essential chromosome'),
  ('a7b7c23f-b288-4f27-9540-1b9061838723', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributes a eukaryotic membrane-bound DNA compartment to a prokaryote'),
  ('a7b7c23f-b288-4f27-9540-1b9061838723', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'names a real prokaryotic surface structure that contains no DNA');

-- [Cell Cycle Phases & Interphase] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher labels the DNA of individual cells with a fluorescent stain that gives a signal proportional to the total amount of DNA present, then images the same cells repeatedly over six months. In a mature neuron, the DNA signal stays constant at the level of an unreplicated diploid genome throughout the entire observation period and never rises, even though the neuron remains metabolically active and continues firing action potentials. Based on these measurements, in which stage of the cell cycle is the neuron?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "G1 phase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "G0 phase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "G2 phase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "S phase"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' DNA content is a fingerprint for cell-cycle position: cells in G1 have a single, unreplicated copy of the genome; the amount of DNA doubles during S phase as replication produces sister chromatids; and cells that have finished S sit at the doubled amount through G2 and into mitosis. The G0 phase is a resting phase for cells that have temporarily or permanently ceased dividing, such as mature nerve cells, which therefore keep an unreplicated diploid genome indefinitely without ever entering S. Here the neuron''s DNA signal is fixed at the unreplicated level and never rises across six months, so it is neither replicating nor holding a doubled genome, and it is not progressing toward replication despite remaining metabolically active. (Choice A) is incorrect: a G1 cell also carries an unreplicated genome, but G1 is a transient growth phase from which a cycling cell moves into S within hours to days; a genome that stays unreplicated for six months without ever advancing to replication is not simply lingering in G1. (Choice B) is correct: the constant, unreplicated DNA content maintained over months in an active-but-non-dividing neuron matches G0, the resting stage occupied by cells that have permanently stopped dividing. (Choice C) is incorrect: a G2 cell has already completed S phase, so its DNA signal would be at the doubled level, twice what is measured here. (Choice D) is incorrect: during S phase the DNA signal would be actively rising toward the doubled amount, whereas the measured signal never changes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between DNA content and cell-cycle stage to the measured data and infer that a stably unreplicated, non-progressing genome in a permanently non-dividing cell indicates \(G_0\).',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '77723537-f327-437e-8ff8-20595e016ede';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '77723537-f327-437e-8ff8-20595e016ede';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('77723537-f327-437e-8ff8-20595e016ede', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses G0 with G1 because both have unreplicated (1x) DNA content and appear as a ''gap'''),
  ('77723537-f327-437e-8ff8-20595e016ede', 'C', 'misconception', 'ignores that G2 follows S and thus has doubled (2x) DNA, mismatching the constant unreplicated signal'),
  ('77723537-f327-437e-8ff8-20595e016ede', 'D', 'misconception', 'picks the actively-replicating phase despite the DNA signal never rising');

-- [Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A single primary spermatocyte begins meiosis. During anaphase of meiosis I, one homologous chromosome pair undergoes nondisjunction, while every other pair separates normally; both meiosis II divisions then proceed without error. Of the four sperm ultimately produced from this one spermatocyte, how many carry an abnormal chromosome number?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "All four sperm are abnormal (two carry an extra copy and two lack a copy of the affected chromosome)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Two sperm are abnormal and two are normal (one abnormal sperm gains a copy and one loses a copy)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "One sperm is abnormal and three are normal (only the cell that received both homologs is affected)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Three sperm are abnormal and one is normal (only a single sperm escapes the mis-segregation)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO).'' Because meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes, a failure to separate at anaphase I sends both members of the pair into one secondary spermatocyte and leaves the other secondary spermatocyte with neither, so both meiosis I products already carry an abnormal number of that chromosome before meiosis II begins. Each secondary spermatocyte then divides normally in meiosis II, faithfully copying its (abnormal) chromosome content into two sperm. OpenStax notes that when homologous chromosomes fail to separate during meiosis I the outcome is two gametes with two copies and two gametes lacking that chromosome. (Choice A) is correct: the one spermatocyte yields four sperm, and because the error occurred before the cell split into two secondary spermatocytes, every one of the four is abnormal — two receive an extra copy (n+1) and two receive none (n−1). (Choice B) is incorrect: a two-normal, two-abnormal outcome results from nondisjunction in meiosis II, where only one secondary spermatocyte''s sister chromatids fail to separate while the other divides correctly; here the failure is in meiosis I, so no normal sperm are produced. (Choice C) is incorrect: although both homologs do enter a single secondary spermatocyte, that cell still divides in meiosis II to produce two n+1 sperm, and the empty secondary spermatocyte produces two n−1 sperm, so far more than one sperm is affected. (Choice D) is incorrect: an even split between gain and loss is required because the two homologs and their absence are partitioned symmetrically into the two secondary spermatocytes, giving 2 and 2 rather than 3 and 1. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the timing of homolog separation in meiosis I to trace how a single mis-segregation propagates through both subsequent divisions and predict the final ratio of abnormal gametes.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'bcab131f-fcca-436a-81ee-4886fee27acd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'bcab131f-fcca-436a-81ee-4886fee27acd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('bcab131f-fcca-436a-81ee-4886fee27acd', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses meiosis I nondisjunction outcome with meiosis II nondisjunction outcome'),
  ('bcab131f-fcca-436a-81ee-4886fee27acd', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes only the cell receiving both homologs, or only one gamete, becomes abnormal'),
  ('bcab131f-fcca-436a-81ee-4886fee27acd', 'D', 'misconception', 'picks an asymmetric 3:1 split instead of the symmetric gain/loss partition');

-- [Spermatogenesis] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A histologist stains a cross-section of a single human seminiferous tubule and notices that the cells are layered: one population hugs the outer basement membrane at the periphery of the tubule, while progressively different cell types are arranged in bands leading inward, with fully tailed cells nearest the central lumen. A geneticist then asks which cells in this section are the diploid stem cells that repopulate the lineage and remain present throughout adult life. Which layer, defined by its position in the tubule, contains the cells the geneticist is asking about?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The outermost layer, resting directly against the basement membrane at the periphery of the tubule"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The layer of large cells one tier inward from the periphery, actively completing the first meiotic division"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The layer just outside the lumen, made of small round cells that are elongating and shedding cytoplasm"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The tailed cells released into the central lumen, ready to enter the duct system"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' Within a seminiferous tubule, germ cells are spatially organized as a maturation gradient: the least mature cells line the basement membrane at the periphery, and cells become progressively more mature as they move inward toward the central lumen, which receives the finished, tailed sperm. OpenStax notes that "the least mature cells, the spermatogonia, line the basement membrane inside the tubule" and that "spermatogonia are the stem cells of the testis," diploid cells that divide to yield spermatocytes, then spermatids, then formed sperm. Because the geneticist asks for the diploid stem cells that persist and repopulate the lineage throughout adult life, the correct layer is the one at the basement membrane. (Choice A) is correct: the outermost layer against the basement membrane holds the spermatogonia—the diploid, self-renewing stem cells—so it is the population the geneticist describes. (Choice B) is incorrect: cells one tier inward completing the first meiotic division are primary spermatocytes; they are derived from spermatogonia and are transiently undergoing meiosis, not the persistent diploid stem cells. (Choice C) is incorrect: small round cells near the lumen that are elongating and shedding cytoplasm are spermatids undergoing spermiogenesis; these are haploid post-meiotic cells, not stem cells. (Choice D) is incorrect: tailed cells released into the lumen are formed (haploid) sperm, the differentiated end product that leaves the testis for the duct system, not a self-renewing stem population. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the basement-membrane-to-lumen maturation gradient of the seminiferous tubule to locate the diploid, self-renewing spermatogonia from their position rather than from a stated definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a9d7d605-0e33-46ee-a635-38bcf58c708c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a9d7d605-0e33-46ee-a635-38bcf58c708c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a9d7d605-0e33-46ee-a635-38bcf58c708c', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'confusing an intermediate meiotic stage (primary spermatocyte) with the stem-cell stage'),
  ('a9d7d605-0e33-46ee-a635-38bcf58c708c', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'mistaking a maturing haploid spermatid for a stem cell'),
  ('a9d7d605-0e33-46ee-a635-38bcf58c708c', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'picking the differentiated end product instead of the self-renewing origin');

-- [Spermatogenesis] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During spermatogenesis, a researcher labels one diploid cell at the basement membrane and tracks its descendants, measuring the DNA content and chromosome number of each cell as the lineage advances toward the tubule lumen. She notes that chromosome number stays at 46 across several successive cells before abruptly dropping to 23 in the next cell she samples. Which transition did she just capture, and what is the identity of that first 23-chromosome cell?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A mitotic division; the first 23-chromosome cell is a second-generation spermatogonium"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The completion of meiosis I; the first 23-chromosome cell is a secondary spermatocyte"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The completion of meiosis II; the first 23-chromosome cell is a spermatid"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Spermiogenesis; the first 23-chromosome cell is a spermatozoon"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' Spermatogenesis begins with mitosis of diploid (2n, 46-chromosome) spermatogonia, which renews the stem-cell pool and produces primary spermatocytes; because these divisions are mitotic, chromosome number stays at 46. Ploidy first drops from 2n to 1n (46 to 23 chromosomes) at meiosis I, when homologous chromosomes separate and a primary spermatocyte yields two secondary spermatocytes. (Choice A) is incorrect: spermatogonial divisions are mitotic and produce diploid daughter cells, so a second-generation spermatogonium still has 46 chromosomes, not 23. (Choice B) is correct: the first cell with 23 chromosomes appears when meiosis I completes and homologs separate, and that cell is by definition the secondary spermatocyte. (Choice C) is incorrect: meiosis II acts on cells that are already haploid (secondary spermatocytes with 23 chromosomes) and produces spermatids, so it does not mark the first drop from 46 to 23. (Choice D) is incorrect: spermiogenesis is the maturation of an already-haploid spermatid into a spermatozoon with no further reduction division, so it changes cell shape, not chromosome number. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between mitotic and meiotic divisions in the spermatogenic lineage to identify where ploidy first falls from 2n to 1n and name the resulting cell.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f698804a-d164-4708-bbe6-03b6b1a0eaa2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f698804a-d164-4708-bbe6-03b6b1a0eaa2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f698804a-d164-4708-bbe6-03b6b1a0eaa2', 'A', 'misconception', 'believes an early lineage division is where ploidy drops / confuses mitosis with reduction division'),
  ('f698804a-d164-4708-bbe6-03b6b1a0eaa2', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'names the correct product of the wrong meiotic division (meiosis II / spermatid) that occurs one step later'),
  ('f698804a-d164-4708-bbe6-03b6b1a0eaa2', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'cites a real lineage step (spermiogenesis) that involves no ploidy change');

-- [Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A mutation disables the motile cilia on the apical surface of the epithelium-like cells that face the fluid-filled cavities of the brain. Affected infants develop enlarged ventricles because the fluid within them no longer circulates and instead pools, raising intracranial pressure. Which central nervous system glial cell is directly impaired by this mutation, and why does the defect produce fluid stagnation?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Microglia, because as the resident macrophages of the CNS they normally clear debris from the ventricles, and losing their motility lets waste accumulate in the fluid"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Astrocytes, because their end-feet regulate the extracellular fluid volume around the ventricles, and without ciliary beating that fluid is no longer reabsorbed"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ependymal cells, because their beating cilia propel the cerebrospinal fluid they produce through the ventricular system, so losing cilia halts its circulation"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oligodendrocytes, because the myelin they form lines the ventricular walls, and its loss removes the surface along which the fluid is normally driven"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' Ependymal cells form a single epithelium-like layer lining the ventricles, and OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e notes they "have cilia on their apical surface to help move the CSF through the ventricular space." Because these same cells produce cerebrospinal fluid at the choroid plexus and then propel it with their cilia, disabling those cilia lets the fluid pool and dilate the ventricles rather than circulate. (Choice A) is incorrect: microglia are the CNS-resident macrophages that "scavenge and degrade dead cells and protect the brain from invading microorganisms," an immune-surveillance role; they do not line the ventricles with motile cilia, so a ciliary mutation would not act through them. (Choice B) is incorrect: astrocytes maintain "the concentration of chemicals in the extracellular space" and help form the blood-brain barrier, but they are not the ciliated epithelium lining the ventricular cavities, so they cannot be the cell whose apical cilia drive CSF flow. (Choice C) is correct: ependymal cells are the ciliated, epithelium-like cells lining the ventricles, and their apical cilia beat to move the CSF they make; abolishing that beating stops circulation and causes the fluid to accumulate under pressure, exactly the phenotype described. (Choice D) is incorrect: oligodendrocytes wrap CNS axons in myelin to insulate them; myelin is a lipid sheath on axons, not a ventricular lining, so its loss would impair nerve conduction rather than CSF movement. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of ependymal cell structure—that these cells line the ventricles and bear motile cilia—to predict which glial cell''s defect halts CSF circulation, rather than recognizing a restated definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '91783113-f0b7-449d-b44a-9915b8b75463';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '91783113-f0b7-449d-b44a-9915b8b75463';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('91783113-f0b7-449d-b44a-9915b8b75463', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct function attributed to wrong glial cell'),
  ('91783113-f0b7-449d-b44a-9915b8b75463', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflating astrocyte fluid/BBB role with CSF circulation'),
  ('91783113-f0b7-449d-b44a-9915b8b75463', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'real oligodendrocyte myelin function misapplied to ventricular lining');

-- [Fertilization] ans=B hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studies polyspermy defenses in newly fertilized eggs. In one experiment, she chemically depletes the egg''s cortical granules before insemination but leaves the plasma membrane and all of its ion channels fully functional. When a sperm then binds, she records that additional sperm are still repelled from fusing during the first second or two, but by roughly 30 seconds later multiple extra sperm have fused and polyspermy occurs. Which defense accounts for the brief early protection that survived the granule depletion, and on what does it depend?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The cortical reaction, which persisted because the discharged granule contents rapidly hardened the zona pellucida into an impenetrable fertilization membrane."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The fast block, which persisted because it is an electrical event driven by a rapid shift in the egg''s membrane potential rather than by granule release."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The slow block, which persisted because the mucopolysaccharide coat it deposits does not require intact cortical granules to form."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The acrosomal reaction, which persisted because the sperm-derived digestive enzymes that clear the zona are unaffected by conditions inside the egg."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' The egg mounts two defenses against polyspermy that differ in speed and mechanism: a fast block, in which the egg''s membrane potential changes almost instantaneously the moment the first sperm binds (Ernest Everett Just''s "wave of negativity"), electrically preventing further fusion; and a slow block, in which the egg alters its membrane structure by discharging cortical granules, building a fertilization membrane over roughly the next half-minute. Because the fast block is purely electrical and the slow block is powered by granule exocytosis, depleting the granules while leaving the membrane''s electrical machinery (its ion channels) intact should knock out only the slow, structural defense. (Choice A) is incorrect: hardening the zona into a fertilization membrane is the granule-dependent slow block, so depleting cortical granules abolishes it — it cannot be the defense that survived. (Choice B) is correct: the only defense that operates within the first second or two is the fast block, and because it is an electrical change in membrane potential rather than a product of granule release, it remains fully functional after granule depletion — exactly matching the observed brief early protection followed by later polyspermy once the (now-absent) slow block should have taken over. (Choice C) is incorrect: the mucopolysaccharide coating that forms the fertilization membrane is deposited by the cortical granules themselves, so it is precisely what fails when granules are depleted, and it is a slow (~30 s) process, not a first-second one. (Choice D) is incorrect: the acrosomal reaction is a sperm event that discharges enzymes to breach the zona so fusion can occur — it promotes fertilization rather than blocking polyspermy, so it is not an egg defense at all. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the electrical fast block and the granule-driven slow block to predict, from a manipulation that removes granules while sparing the membrane''s ion channels, which defense remains and why.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '774ad094-5762-445d-8648-09cbebf96132';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '774ad094-5762-445d-8648-09cbebf96132';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('774ad094-5762-445d-8648-09cbebf96132', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributes the surviving defense to the very granule-dependent structural block that the manipulation ablated'),
  ('774ad094-5762-445d-8648-09cbebf96132', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes the slow-block mucopolysaccharide coat forms independently of cortical granules'),
  ('774ad094-5762-445d-8648-09cbebf96132', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'confuses the sperm''s acrosomal reaction with an egg polyspermy block');

-- [Specification & Determination] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two vertebrate embryos are compared at an early cleavage stage. In Embryo 1, a researcher removes a single blastomere; the remaining cells reorganize and the embryo develops into a complete, normal larva. In Embryo 2, removing the analogous blastomere yields a larva that permanently lacks a specific set of body structures, and no neighboring cell replaces the missing region. Which conclusion about the cells at the moment of removal is best supported by these results?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "In Embryo 2 the removed cell''s developmental fate was already fixed, so no other cell could substitute for the structures it alone was assigned to form."}, {"label": "B", "text": "In Embryo 1 the removed cell''s fate was already fixed, which is why the surrounding cells were free to rebuild the whole organism without it."}, {"label": "C", "text": "In Embryo 2 the removed cell had already completed differentiation into its final specialized form, which is what allowed the rest of the embryo to develop normally."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Because cells in both embryos were actively dividing, the cells in both must have been equally committed to their fates at the moment of removal."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Mechanisms of Development.'' Determination is the point at which a cell''s developmental fate becomes fixed; once a cell is determined, no other cell can take over the fate it was committed to. The textbook illustrates this with cleavage experiments: in embryos whose cells are already determined ("determinate cleavage"), "removal of a blastomere ... can result in missing structures," whereas in embryos whose cells are not yet committed ("indeterminate cleavage"), "removal of individual blastomeres ... does not result in the loss of embryonic structures" because adjacent cells compensate, and separated blastomeres can even form twins. (Choice A) is correct: a permanently missing, non-replaceable structure after removal is the signature of a cell whose fate was already fixed—the other cells cannot make what that cell alone was assigned to produce, so those cells were determined. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the logic. Embryo 1''s cells recovered fully precisely because their fates were NOT yet fixed, letting neighbors reorganize and replace the missing cell; a fixed fate would have prevented that compensation. (Choice C) is incorrect: completing differentiation is the later acquisition of a specialized structure and function, not the commitment event, and a fully differentiated removed cell would not explain why the surrounding cells could rebuild normally—that outcome describes Embryo 1, whose cells were still uncommitted. (Choice D) is incorrect: ongoing cell division (cleavage) is mitotic partitioning and says nothing about whether fate is fixed; the differing experimental outcomes show the two embryos'' cells were NOT equally committed, so shared division activity cannot establish equal determination. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of determination to interpret blastomere-removal results and infer which embryo''s cells had fixed fates.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '14d082a0-5487-47e7-930c-5e263b22bec2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '14d082a0-5487-47e7-930c-5e263b22bec2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('14d082a0-5487-47e7-930c-5e263b22bec2', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Attributes the fixed fate to the embryo that fully recovered, inverting the cause-effect between determination and regulative compensation'),
  ('14d082a0-5487-47e7-930c-5e263b22bec2', 'C', 'misconception', 'Confuses determination (fate commitment) with differentiation (visible specialization)'),
  ('14d082a0-5487-47e7-930c-5e263b22bec2', 'D', 'misconception', 'Treats shared cell-division activity as evidence of equal fate commitment, ignoring the differing experimental outcomes');

-- [Differentiation & Stem-Cell Potency] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'To distinguish true stem cells from other dividing cells in a bone-marrow sample, a researcher fluorescently labels several individual candidate cells, tracks each one''s descendants over many weeks in culture, and records what those descendants become. The four cells below each divide at least once. Which single-cell lineage-tracing result identifies its founder cell as a stem cell rather than a progenitor or a terminally differentiated cell?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The founder divides a few times, and every descendant becomes a mature red blood cell, after which the lineage stops dividing entirely"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The founder repeatedly produces daughters that are identical to itself while also yielding daughters that mature into several blood cell types, and this output continues indefinitely"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The founder divides many times but every descendant remains identical to the founder, none ever maturing into a specialized cell type"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The founder produces daughters that mature into several blood cell types, but the founder-like cells are used up within a few divisions and are never regenerated"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Mechanisms of Development.'' A stem cell is defined by two properties working together: self-renewal (it can divide without limit and regenerate more cells identical to itself) and the potency to differentiate, under specific conditions, into one or more specialized cell types. The textbook notes that stem cells "continually divide and regenerate new stem cells instead of further specializing," and that hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow both persist and "differentiate into" the various blood cell types. A correct lineage trace must therefore show BOTH continuous self-renewal AND ongoing production of specialized cells; a cell showing only one of these is a progenitor, a terminal cell, or a non-differentiating proliferator. (Choice A) is incorrect: a founder whose descendants all become one mature cell type and then stop dividing has differentiated and lost self-renewal—this is a terminally differentiated (or short-lived progenitor) lineage, not a stem cell. (Choice B) is correct: it uniquely captures both defining features—the founder keeps regenerating cells identical to itself (self-renewal, "divide without limit") while also giving rise to several specialized cell types (multilineage differentiation)—and does so indefinitely, which is exactly the behavior expected of a stem cell. (Choice C) is incorrect: unlimited division with no descendant ever specializing shows self-renewal but no differentiation potency, so the potency criterion of a stem cell is not met. (Choice D) is incorrect: producing multiple specialized cell types satisfies potency, but because the founder-like cells are exhausted and never regenerated, self-renewal fails—this describes a multipotent progenitor, not a stem cell. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the twin criteria of self-renewal and differentiation to interpret single-cell lineage-tracing data and select the outcome that satisfies both.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c501f3e9-2fa4-4d58-97c9-10fba7032837';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c501f3e9-2fa4-4d58-97c9-10fba7032837';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c501f3e9-2fa4-4d58-97c9-10fba7032837', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses transient proliferation of a differentiated/progenitor lineage with stem-cell behavior'),
  ('c501f3e9-2fa4-4d58-97c9-10fba7032837', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats unlimited division alone as sufficient, ignoring the differentiation requirement'),
  ('c501f3e9-2fa4-4d58-97c9-10fba7032837', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'describes a multipotent progenitor (differentiation without self-renewal) and mistakes it for a stem cell');

-- [Differentiation & Stem-Cell Potency] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A team confirms that reprogrammed skin fibroblasts differentiate in culture into functional neurons, cardiac muscle, and pancreatic acinar cells. They then compare these reprogrammed cells against a freshly isolated zygote in two assays: (1) injection into a host blastocyst to test which lineages each can contribute to, and (2) an aggregation test of whether either cell type, acting alone, can generate a complete, self-sustaining conceptus. Based on the culture result alone, which single outcome should the team predict for the reprogrammed cells relative to the zygote?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "In the chimera assay the reprogrammed cells will populate embryonic tissues but not the placental trophoblast, and unlike the zygote they will fail the standalone-conceptus test"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The reprogrammed cells and the zygote will behave identically in both assays, each contributing to placenta as well as embryo and each independently yielding a complete conceptus"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The reprogrammed cells will contribute only to ectodermal derivatives such as the nervous system, because a fibroblast origin restricts them to their germ layer of origin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The reprogrammed cells will match the zygote in the standalone test but, unlike the zygote, will be excluded from mesodermal tissues such as cardiac muscle in the chimera"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Mechanisms of Development.'' A cell''s potency class predicts exactly which lineages it can and cannot build. OpenStax Biology 2e notes that in the blastocyst the inner cell mass "will go on to form the embryo," while the separate trophoblast layer "will contribute to the placenta"; the reprogrammed cells'' demonstrated ability to make neurons (ectoderm), cardiac muscle (mesoderm), and pancreatic acinar cells (endoderm) marks them as pluripotent, an inner-cell-mass-like state, whereas only the totipotent zygote can build both the embryo and the extraembryonic trophoblast and support a whole organism on its own. (Choice A) is correct: because the cells already generate derivatives of all three germ layers, they sit at the pluripotent rung, so in a chimera they contribute broadly to the embryo proper yet cannot form placental trophoblast, and they lack the zygote''s totipotent capacity to build a complete conceptus alone. (Choice B) is incorrect: identical behavior would require the reprogrammed cells to be totipotent like the zygote, but forming all three germ layers in culture does not confer the trophoblast-forming or whole-organism capacity that separates totipotency from pluripotency. (Choice C) is incorrect: the culture result explicitly shows mesodermal (cardiac) and endodermal (pancreatic) derivatives, so the cells are not confined to their fibroblast/ectodermal origin; reprogramming erases that lineage restriction. (Choice D) is incorrect: it inverts the true limits, granting the cells a standalone-organism ability they lack while wrongly excluding them from mesoderm, the very lineage the cardiac-muscle result demonstrates they can form. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the potency hierarchy to a culture result and predict two distinct assay outcomes, distinguishing the embryo-only reach of a pluripotent cell from the trophoblast-plus-whole-organism reach of the totipotent zygote.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e4ef2bf4-50de-4cae-b22d-11c19d7bea95';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e4ef2bf4-50de-4cae-b22d-11c19d7bea95';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e4ef2bf4-50de-4cae-b22d-11c19d7bea95', 'B', 'misconception', 'treats all-three-germ-layer ability as equivalent to totipotency'),
  ('e4ef2bf4-50de-4cae-b22d-11c19d7bea95', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes reprogrammed cells retain their somatic germ-layer-of-origin restriction'),
  ('e4ef2bf4-50de-4cae-b22d-11c19d7bea95', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps which capability (standalone organism vs mesoderm reach) the cells actually have');

-- [Cell-Cell Communication] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A tumor-derived cell line proliferates in serum-free medium. To identify how its dividing signal is delivered, researchers run three conditions: (1) cells plated at normal density; (2) cells plated at extremely low ("clonal") density so that each cell is physically isolated, with hundreds of micrometers of empty medium between it and any neighbor; and (3) clonal-density cells grown in a chamber that continuously perfuses fresh medium past each cell, immediately washing away anything the cell releases. The cells proliferate vigorously in conditions (1) and (2) but stop dividing in condition (3). Which mode of chemical signaling is most consistent with all three results?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Paracrine signaling"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Endocrine signaling"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Juxtacrine (direct contact) signaling"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Autocrine signaling"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Mechanisms of Development.'' The four modes of chemical signaling differ chiefly in the distance a ligand travels and in the identity of the responding cell: paracrine signals diffuse a short distance to nearby cells, endocrine signals travel through the bloodstream to distant cells, direct-contact (juxtacrine) signals require a physically touching neighbor, and autocrine signals are received by the same cell that released them. Because proliferation survives when each cell is isolated with no neighbors (condition 2) but collapses only when the released ligand is instantly washed away (condition 3), the responding cell must be the secreting cell itself. (Choice A) is incorrect: paracrine signaling requires a nearby responding cell reached by local diffusion, so isolating each cell hundreds of micrometers apart (condition 2) should have abolished division—yet it did not, ruling paracrine out. (Choice B) is incorrect: endocrine signaling delivers hormones over long distances through the circulatory system to distant targets, a route absent in a perfused culture dish; it also cannot explain why a single isolated cell keeps dividing. (Choice C) is incorrect: juxtacrine signaling needs a directly contacting neighbor cell, but proliferation persisted at clonal density where cells never touch (condition 2). (Choice D) is correct: only a cell responding to a ligand it releases itself can keep dividing while completely isolated yet fail the instant that secreted ligand is perfused away—the defining features of autocrine signaling, a mode common in early development and in tumor growth. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distance-and-target logic of each signaling mode to a set of density and perfusion manipulations and eliminate the modes each result contradicts, rather than recall a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '9d177a4d-d8d0-43b7-88b3-6c0a4a2b5f4b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9d177a4d-d8d0-43b7-88b3-6c0a4a2b5f4b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9d177a4d-d8d0-43b7-88b3-6c0a4a2b5f4b', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses self-signaling with short-range neighbor signaling'),
  ('9d177a4d-d8d0-43b7-88b3-6c0a4a2b5f4b', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'long-distance hormonal signaling irrelevant to isolated cultured cells'),
  ('9d177a4d-d8d0-43b7-88b3-6c0a4a2b5f4b', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes membrane-anchored contact signaling despite isolation persisting');

-- [Cell-Cell Communication] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During a neuroscience unit, two students argue about how to classify chemical transmission at a synapse. They agree on the mechanics: an electrical impulse reaching the axon terminal triggers release of neurotransmitter molecules, which cross the ~30 nm cleft between two distinct nerve cells, bind receptors on the next cell, and are then destroyed by enzymes in the cleft or pumped back into the releasing terminal. One student insists this counts as its own separate category because it happens between neurons; the other argues it is actually the same fundamental signaling category the body uses for many short-range chemical messages. Which classification is correct, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "It is paracrine signaling, because the neurotransmitter is a secreted ligand that diffuses locally through the extracellular space to reach a neighboring cell, so it belongs to the same general signaling category as other short-range secreted messengers rather than forming a class of its own."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It is endocrine signaling, because the neurotransmitter is a chemical messenger released from one cell to change the activity of another cell, which is the general definition shared by all hormone-based communication."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It is autocrine signaling, because a neuron both releases the neurotransmitter and expresses receptors that respond to it, so the signaling cell is effectively acting on its own cell type."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It is direct signaling through gap junctions, because the extremely small distance between the two neurons lets the chemical messenger move straight from one cytoplasm into the next without being released outside the cell."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Mechanisms of Development.'' The four categories of chemical signaling differ mainly in how far the signal travels and how it gets from source to target: paracrine signals diffuse locally through the extracellular matrix to nearby cells, endocrine signals are hormones carried long distances by the bloodstream, autocrine signals act back on the releasing cell, and gap-junction signals pass directly between adjacent cytoplasms without entering the extracellular space. OpenStax explicitly presents chemical synaptic transmission as "an example of paracrine signaling," because the neurotransmitter is a secreted ligand that diffuses across the narrow cleft and is then quickly degraded or reabsorbed. (Choice A) is correct: the classification rests on the mechanism of transport, not on the cell type involved — because the neurotransmitter is a secreted molecule that diffuses through the extracellular space to a neighboring cell, synaptic transmission falls under paracrine signaling, the same general category as other short-range secreted messengers, rather than a class of its own. (Choice B) is incorrect: although a neurotransmitter is a released chemical messenger, endocrine signaling specifically requires a hormone carried "via the bloodstream" over large distances, where it "become[s] diluted"; the messenger here crosses only a ~30 nm gap, so distance and transport route rule endocrine out. (Choice C) is incorrect: autocrine signaling requires that the "signaling cell and the target cell can be the same," but the scenario states the neurotransmitter acts on a distinct downstream nerve cell across the cleft, not back on the releasing terminal. (Choice D) is incorrect: gap-junction signaling moves mediators "directly between" cells through channels linking their cytoplasms, whereas the neurotransmitter here is released into and diffuses across the extracellular cleft before binding surface receptors. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the criteria that distinguish the four signaling categories to a described mechanism and infer the correct classification, rather than recalling a definition that is handed to you.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e0c77e2f-aa73-4846-b3f0-2f0c44216866';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e0c77e2f-aa73-4846-b3f0-2f0c44216866';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e0c77e2f-aa73-4846-b3f0-2f0c44216866', 'B', 'misconception', 'Treats any cell-to-cell chemical messenger as endocrine by ignoring the required bloodstream transport and long-distance criterion'),
  ('e0c77e2f-aa73-4846-b3f0-2f0c44216866', 'C', 'misconception', 'Confuses the fact that neurons can express receptors for their own transmitters with true self-targeting autocrine signaling'),
  ('e0c77e2f-aa73-4846-b3f0-2f0c44216866', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'Assumes the very small synaptic gap means direct cytoplasmic (gap-junction) coupling rather than extracellular diffusion');

-- [Placental Functions] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher samples blood from two vessels of the umbilical cord of a healthy full-term fetus. Blood in the vessel carrying blood away from the placenta and toward the fetus (the umbilical vein) has a partial pressure of oxygen (\(P_{O_2}\)) of about 30 mmHg, whereas blood in the vessels returning to the placenta from the fetus (the umbilical arteries) has a \(P_{O_2}\) of about 20 mmHg. Maternal blood in the placental sinuses has a \(P_{O_2}\) of about 50 mmHg. Which mechanism best accounts for the fetal blood gaining oxygen as it passes through the placenta?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Oxygen moves by passive diffusion from maternal blood into fetal blood, driven by the higher \\(P_{O_2}\\) on the maternal side of the placental barrier"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Placental transporter proteins actively pump oxygen into fetal capillaries against its partial-pressure gradient using ATP"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fetal blood is oxygenated by the fetal lungs and then routed through the umbilical vein to the placenta for storage"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Maternal immunoglobulins crossing the placenta bind and carry oxygen molecules directly into the fetal circulation"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Gas exchange at the placenta follows the same principle as gas exchange in the lung: it occurs by diffusion, a process in which transport is "driven by a concentration gradient," so gas molecules "move from a region of high concentration to a region of low concentration," and "the rate of diffusion of a gas is proportional to its partial pressure." In utero the fetal lungs are compressed, filled with amniotic fluid, and nonfunctional, so the placenta—which "functions in gas exchange and waste management"—is the site of respiration; maternal blood (\(P_{O_2}\) ~50 mmHg) is more oxygen-rich than the returning fetal blood (\(P_{O_2}\) ~20 mmHg), establishing the gradient that loads oxygen into the fetal blood leaving in the umbilical vein (\(P_{O_2}\) ~30 mmHg). (Choice A) is correct: oxygen crosses the placental barrier by passive diffusion down its partial-pressure gradient, from the higher-\(P_{O_2}\) maternal blood to the lower-\(P_{O_2}\) fetal blood, exactly as the data show. (Choice B) is incorrect: while the placenta does use active, ATP-dependent transport for some solutes such as certain amino acids, oxygen is small and lipid-soluble and moves passively down its gradient; no pump is needed, and the maternal-to-fetal direction here is with the gradient, not against it. (Choice C) is incorrect: the fetal lungs are fluid-filled and nonfunctional before birth and perform no gas exchange, and the umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood toward the fetus, not toward the placenta for storage. (Choice D) is incorrect: maternal immunoglobulins do cross the placenta to provide passive immunity, but antibodies do not transport oxygen; oxygen carriage in blood is a function of hemoglobin within red blood cells, not of immunoglobulins. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that gases diffuse down their partial-pressure gradient to interpret the measured \(P_{O_2}\) values and identify the transport mechanism.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b7acaa10-34e9-431e-96e0-7f5b5477e141';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b7acaa10-34e9-431e-96e0-7f5b5477e141';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b7acaa10-34e9-431e-96e0-7f5b5477e141', 'B', 'misconception', 'assuming solute transport across the placenta is always active/ATP-driven'),
  ('b7acaa10-34e9-431e-96e0-7f5b5477e141', 'C', 'misconception', 'believing fetal lungs oxygenate blood in utero'),
  ('b7acaa10-34e9-431e-96e0-7f5b5477e141', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'conflating the placenta''s passive-immunity function with its respiratory function');

-- [Myelin & Myelinating Glia] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a spinal cord white-matter sample, a researcher fills a single glial cell with a fluorescent tracer and reconstructs it in three dimensions. The tracer fills the cell body and several thin extensions, and the tips of those extensions are found forming lipid-rich insulating wraps on five different axon segments belonging to three separate neurons. Which glial cell type is most consistent with this reconstruction?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Schwann cell"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Oligodendrocyte"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Astrocyte"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Microglial cell"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' Myelinating glia insulate axons in a lipid-rich sheath, but the two types differ in architecture: a Schwann cell (PNS) surrounds only a single segment of a single axon, whereas an oligodendrocyte (CNS) sends out a few branching processes that each myelinate a separate axon segment, so one cell can service segments on several different neurons. The reconstruction here is in spinal cord white matter (central nervous system) and shows one cell body wrapping five segments across three neurons, so the number of segments served and the location together point to only one cell type. (Choice A) is incorrect: the Schwann cell is a genuine myelinating glial cell, but it operates in the peripheral nervous system, not the spinal cord, and each Schwann cell wraps a portion of only one axon segment—so it could never account for one cell insulating five segments on three neurons. (Choice B) is correct: the oligodendrocyte is the CNS myelinating glial cell whose several processes each reach out to a different axon segment, allowing a single cell to insulate multiple segments across separate axons, exactly the branching one-to-many pattern reconstructed here in central nervous tissue. (Choice C) is incorrect: the astrocyte is a real CNS glial cell, but it supports neurons by regulating extracellular ions, clearing signaling molecules, and forming the blood-brain barrier; it does not produce myelin, so it would not appear as insulating wraps on axon segments. (Choice D) is incorrect: the microglial cell is a real CNS glial cell, but it acts as a resident macrophage that scavenges and degrades dead cells and pathogens; it performs immune surveillance rather than myelination and would not form lipid sheaths on axons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the one-cell-to-many-segments architecture of CNS myelinating glia, together with the central-nervous-system location, to infer which glial cell type produced the reconstructed pattern.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '69d55470-b690-4b59-a278-de4f21f8c3d3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '69d55470-b690-4b59-a278-de4f21f8c3d3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('69d55470-b690-4b59-a278-de4f21f8c3d3', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'Correct that it myelinates, wrong compartment and wrong architecture (PNS, one segment per cell)'),
  ('69d55470-b690-4b59-a278-de4f21f8c3d3', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'Genuine CNS glia but supports/BBB, does not myelinate'),
  ('69d55470-b690-4b59-a278-de4f21f8c3d3', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'Genuine CNS glia but immune scavenger, does not myelinate');

-- [Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia] ans=B hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A child is diagnosed with a genetic condition in which the axonemal motor proteins of motile cilia are nonfunctional throughout the body. In addition to chronic airway congestion, imaging shows that cerebrospinal fluid, though still being produced at normal volume, is failing to circulate onward through the ventricular system and is instead pooling within the ventricles. A neurologist attributes the CSF-flow problem to impaired function of one CNS glial population. Which glial cell is responsible, and why does the cilia defect specifically disrupt CSF movement rather than CSF production?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Astrocytes; their perivascular end-feet normally beat CSF forward, so losing ciliary motion stalls flow without affecting the volume filtered from blood."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ependymal cells; the apical cilia of this ventricular lining propel CSF along the ventricular system, whereas its production depends instead on filtration of blood at the choroid plexus."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Microglia; these resident phagocytes use cilia to patrol the ventricles, so their immobilization lets debris accumulate and block CSF from advancing."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oligodendrocytes; the ciliated processes that extend from these cells sweep CSF between myelinated tracts, so a cilia defect halts flow while blood filtration continues."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' Among the four CNS glia, only one lines the ventricular cavities as a single epithelium-like layer and bears motile cilia on its apical surface; OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e states these cells "have cilia on their apical surface to help move the CSF through the ventricular space," while CSF itself is generated separately when the same cell type at the choroid plexus "filter[s] the blood to make CSF." Production (blood filtration) and propulsion (ciliary beating) are therefore distinct jobs, so a pure cilia-motility defect can leave CSF volume normal yet strand the fluid in the ventricles. (Choice A) is incorrect: astrocytes send end-feet onto capillaries to buffer extracellular ions and help form the blood-brain barrier, but they are not ciliated and do not line the ventricles, so they cannot be the propulsive element failing here. (Choice B) is correct: ependymal cells form the ciliated ventricular lining whose beating cilia move CSF onward, and because CSF is instead produced by blood filtration at the choroid plexus, an axonemal defect knocks out movement while leaving production intact. (Choice C) is incorrect: microglia are the CNS-resident macrophages that phagocytose pathogens and debris; they are not ciliated and play no role in driving bulk CSF flow, so attributing flow to their cilia is unfounded. (Choice D) is incorrect: oligodendrocytes extend processes that wrap central axons in myelin, not ciliated processes that contact fluid, so they neither move CSF nor bear the motile cilia the defect would impair. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between which glial cell bears motile cilia and which function (fluid propulsion vs. blood filtration) a ciliary defect would selectively disable, rather than recalling a stated definition.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25', 'A', 'misconception', 'confusing astrocyte perivascular contact with a mechanical CSF-propulsion role'),
  ('6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'attributing CSF flow to microglial patrolling rather than ependymal cilia'),
  ('6ba0ec85-ce95-4f26-aae2-3ce410e33d25', 'D', 'misconception', 'extending oligodendrocyte process-extension to a fictional ciliated fluid-sweeping function');

-- [Reflex Arcs] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During a neurology exam, a clinician elicits two spinal reflexes and records the central (within-CNS) delay for each after correcting for the distance signals travel along the peripheral nerves. Tapping the patellar tendon produces a knee jerk with a short central delay, whereas a brief noxious heat applied to the fingertip produces limb withdrawal with a measurably longer central delay. Both reflexes are integrated at the level of the spinal cord and act on skeletal muscle. Which statement BEST accounts for the longer central delay of the withdrawal reflex?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The withdrawal circuit routes the signal through an additional CNS neuron before reaching the motor neuron, so the signal must cross more chemical synapses, each of which adds a delay."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The withdrawal reflex is processed by the autonomic division, and autonomic conduction is intrinsically slower than somatic conduction."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The sensory neuron in the withdrawal reflex is unmyelinated, so its signal reaches the spinal cord more slowly than the myelinated stretch-reflex afferent."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The withdrawal reflex relies on a longer stretch of gray matter, and conduction across gray matter is slower than conduction across white matter."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' Reflex arcs are classified by how many synapses separate the sensory (afferent) input from the motor (efferent) output: the knee-jerk stretch reflex is monosynaptic, meaning the sensory neuron directly activates the motor neuron, whereas the hot-stimulus withdrawal reflex inserts an interneuron in the spinal cord between the sensory and motor neurons (A&P 2e, lines 26846-26849; 28059). Because every chemical synapse imposes a synaptic delay, the added CNS relay in the withdrawal circuit predicts a longer central delay even when peripheral conduction distance is held constant. (Choice A) is correct: the withdrawal (polysynaptic) arc passes through an interposed spinal interneuron, so the signal crosses one more chemical synapse than the direct (monosynaptic) stretch arc, and each synapse contributes an increment of delay—this is the only explanation consistent with the reflexes being matched for conduction distance and both being spinal and somatic. (Choice B) is incorrect because withdrawal is a somatic reflex acting on skeletal muscle, not an autonomic (visceral) reflex, so an autonomic-conduction argument does not apply; the stem specifies both reflexes act on skeletal muscle. (Choice C) is incorrect because it is not the identity of the afferent fiber but the number of central synapses that produces the longer delay, and in any case the stem already corrected for peripheral conduction distance, removing afferent conduction speed as the explanation for the CNS delay difference. (Choice D) is incorrect because axonal conduction velocity is set by fiber diameter and myelination, not by whether the axon runs through gray or white matter; central delay in a reflex is dominated by synaptic transmission across neuron-to-neuron junctions, not by a supposed slow conduction through gray matter. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the monosynaptic-versus-polysynaptic distinction to infer that an extra interposed neuron adds a synapse—and therefore synaptic delay—rather than recall a stated definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2e2d8e12-2e99-4549-8805-09e332763258';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2e2d8e12-2e99-4549-8805-09e332763258';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2e2d8e12-2e99-4549-8805-09e332763258', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing somatic protective reflex with autonomic because the stimulus is noxious/involuntary'),
  ('2e2d8e12-2e99-4549-8805-09e332763258', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'attributing central delay to peripheral afferent conduction speed, which the stem already corrected for'),
  ('2e2d8e12-2e99-4549-8805-09e332763258', 'D', 'misconception', 'believing gray vs. white matter location governs conduction velocity rather than synapse count');

-- [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher bathes an isolated, membrane-permeabilized skeletal muscle fiber in a solution that contains \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) but no nucleotides. The fiber becomes stiff and resists being stretched. Working from this rigid state, which single addition to the bath would most directly allow the fiber to become pliable again?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{ATP}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Additional \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{ADP}\\) plus inorganic phosphate"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) chelator to lower free \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' In the cross-bridge cycle, a myosin head that has completed its power stroke stays attached to actin until a new molecule of ATP binds the head, which is the specific event that causes the cross-bridge to detach. A permeabilized fiber in a \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-rich but nucleotide-free bath therefore forms cross-bridges that can never release, producing a rigid, rigor-like state; this is the same mechanism that underlies rigor mortis when a dead muscle exhausts its \(\text{ATP}\). (Choice A) is correct: because ATP binding is the step that triggers myosin-actin detachment, restoring \(\text{ATP}\) to the bath lets the trapped heads release from actin, break the locked cross-bridges, and free the filaments to slide, so the fiber becomes pliable again. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is already present and its role is to expose actin''s binding sites by shifting tropomyosin, which if anything promotes more cross-bridge formation; adding more \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) cannot detach heads that are stuck for lack of nucleotide. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{ADP}\) and inorganic phosphate are the hydrolysis products present on the head during attachment and the power stroke, not the ligand that drives release; supplying them does not provide the ATP whose binding is required to break the cross-bridge. (Choice D) is incorrect: lowering free \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) would let tropomyosin re-cover the binding sites for heads that are unbound, but heads already locked onto actin remain attached until ATP binds, so chelating \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) cannot relieve the existing rigidity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that ATP binding, not \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) or the hydrolysis products, is what triggers myosin detachment to predict which reagent reverses an experimentally induced rigor state.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c2b35507-d43f-4edc-8607-63c215f40e48';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c2b35507-d43f-4edc-8607-63c215f40e48';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c2b35507-d43f-4edc-8607-63c215f40e48', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses the trigger for contraction (Ca2+) with the trigger for detachment (ATP)'),
  ('c2b35507-d43f-4edc-8607-63c215f40e48', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'cites the hydrolysis products bound during the power stroke rather than the ATP that drives release'),
  ('c2b35507-d43f-4edc-8607-63c215f40e48', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'assumes removing the contraction trigger relaxes already-bound cross-bridges, reversing the ATP requirement for detachment');

-- [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A purified protein is monitored by UV spectroscopy as the solution pH is raised from 7 to 12. Near pH 10.5 the near-UV absorbance shifts (a new signal appears at 295 nm), and this change is fully reversible when the pH is lowered again. The researcher concludes that a single class of side chain is being titrated over this range. Absorbance measurements at 280 nm confirm the protein contains all three aromatic residues in significant amounts. Which residue is most likely responsible for the pH-dependent spectral shift observed near pH 10.5?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Tyrosine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Phenylalanine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tryptophan"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Lysine"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' To produce a reversible, pH-dependent change in near-UV absorbance around pH 10.5, a residue must satisfy two conditions at once: its side chain must ionize (gain or lose a proton) in that pH range, and that ionization must alter a group that absorbs UV light. The LibreTexts reference notes that at high pH ''the side-chain hydroxyl is deprotonated (pKa = 10.5), with concomitant changes in A295,'' and that ''these changes can be used to follow the titration of the Tyr residues in a protein.'' Among the three aromatic residues, only tyrosine carries an ionizable group directly on its UV-absorbing ring. (Choice A) is correct: tyrosine''s phenolic side-chain hydroxyl deprotonates near pH 10.5, and because that hydroxyl sits directly on the aromatic ring, its loss of a proton changes the ring''s absorbance, generating the reversible 295 nm signal used to titrate Tyr residues. (Choice B) is incorrect: phenylalanine absorbs UV light, but its side chain is an unsubstituted benzyl group with no ionizable substituent, so raising the pH to 10.5 does not protonate or deprotonate it and produces no titration signal. (Choice C) is incorrect: tryptophan also absorbs UV strongly, but its indole nitrogen''s lone pair is delocalized into the aromatic ring and is not titratable in this range, so its absorbance does not shift with pH near 10.5. (Choice D) is incorrect: lysine''s ε-amino side chain does ionize near pH 10.5, so it is titrated in this range, but it is aliphatic and has no chromophore, so its deprotonation produces no change in near-UV absorbance. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the requirement that the same residue be both titratable near pH 10.5 and UV-absorbing, integrating side-chain pKa with the spectroscopic observation rather than recalling a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8a8e9aaf-87c2-465e-9b21-90cf355c9f31';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8a8e9aaf-87c2-465e-9b21-90cf355c9f31';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8a8e9aaf-87c2-465e-9b21-90cf355c9f31', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes any aromatic residue that absorbs UV must also be responsible for a pH-linked absorbance shift'),
  ('8a8e9aaf-87c2-465e-9b21-90cf355c9f31', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'knows tryptophan is the strongest UV absorber and wrongly assumes its indole nitrogen is titratable near pH 10.5'),
  ('8a8e9aaf-87c2-465e-9b21-90cf355c9f31', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'correctly recalls lysine''s side-chain pKa is near 10.5 but ignores that lysine has no chromophore to produce a spectral shift');

-- [Side-Chain Classification] ans=D hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During a single catalytic cycle, an enzyme''s active site must first remove a proton from an incoming water molecule (acting as a general base) and then, moments later, donate a proton to the developing oxyanion of the tetrahedral intermediate (acting as a general acid). To perform both roles efficiently at a physiological pH of 7.4, a single side chain should be substantially populated in BOTH its protonated and deprotonated forms under those conditions. Which amino acid''s side chain is best suited to serve as this dual-function proton shuttle?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Aspartate (side-chain pKₐ ≈ 3.9)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Lysine (side-chain pKₐ ≈ 10.5)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Arginine (side-chain pKₐ ≈ 12.5)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Histidine (side-chain pKₐ ≈ 6.5)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' A side chain can shuttle protons — acting as both a general base (accepting a proton) and a general acid (donating one) — only if a meaningful fraction of the residues are in each protonation state at the working pH; by the Henderson–Hasselbalch relationship, that requires a side-chain pKₐ close to the pH, so the ratio of protonated to deprotonated forms is near 1:1. The textbook notes that among the basic residues, lysine and arginine "would always be protonated" at physiological pH, whereas histidine "exists in both protonated and deprotonated states at physiological pH, so it can readily gain a proton and act as a general base" (LibreTexts lines 6117–6120). (Choice A) is incorrect: aspartate is a genuine active-site catalytic residue, but with a pKₐ near 3.9 its carboxyl is essentially fully deprotonated (anionic) at pH 7.4, so it has almost no protonated form available to donate a proton as a general acid. (Choice B) is incorrect: lysine''s ε-amino group has a pKₐ near 10.5, so at pH 7.4 it is nearly completely protonated (–NH₃⁺) and cannot readily give up a proton to act cleanly in both directions. (Choice C) is incorrect: arginine''s guanidinium group (pKₐ ≈ 12.5) is even more strongly biased toward the protonated, positively charged form at physiological pH, leaving essentially no deprotonated population to accept a proton. (Choice D) is correct: histidine''s imidazole side chain has a pKₐ near 6.5, placing pH 7.4 within roughly one unit of its pKₐ, so both the protonated and neutral forms are appreciably populated — this is precisely why histidine is the classic general acid/base catalyst in enzyme active sites. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between side-chain pKₐ and the ambient pH to determine which residue can occupy both protonation states at physiological pH and therefore serve as a proton shuttle.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '29508cff-7416-4c2b-99b5-7554a6f9f923';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '29508cff-7416-4c2b-99b5-7554a6f9f923';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('29508cff-7416-4c2b-99b5-7554a6f9f923', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'acidic active-site residue that is fully deprotonated at physiological pH'),
  ('29508cff-7416-4c2b-99b5-7554a6f9f923', 'B', 'misconception', 'basic residue assumed interchangeable with histidine despite high pKa'),
  ('29508cff-7416-4c2b-99b5-7554a6f9f923', 'C', 'misconception', 'basic residue assumed interchangeable with histidine despite very high pKa');

-- [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A crystallographer characterizes two β-sheet domains, X and Y, that have the same number of strands, the same strand length, and nearly identical amino acid composition. In domain X, each inter-strand backbone hydrogen bond is essentially colinear: the donor amide N–H points almost straight at the acceptor carbonyl O (deviation < 5°). In domain Y, the same backbone hydrogen bonds are noticeably skewed, deviating roughly 20–25° from linear. When the two domains are thermally denatured under identical conditions, domain X unfolds at a distinctly higher melting temperature than domain Y. Which conclusion about the strand arrangement in the two domains is BEST supported by these observations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Domain X is antiparallel and domain Y is parallel, because the optimal (near-linear) hydrogen-bond geometry that stabilizes X is achieved when adjacent strands run in opposite directions."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Domain X is parallel and domain Y is antiparallel, because parallel strands pack more tightly and therefore form the straighter, stronger hydrogen bonds seen in X."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both domains are parallel, and the difference in melting temperature reflects a difference in the number of intra-strand \\(i \\rightarrow i+4\\) hydrogen bonds within each strand."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both domains are antiparallel, and domain X is more stable only because its strands are cross-linked by additional disulfide bonds rather than by any difference in hydrogen-bond geometry."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' In a β-pleated sheet the strands are held together by inter-strand backbone hydrogen bonds between amide H and carbonyl O atoms, but the geometry of those bonds depends on strand direction: when adjacent strands run in opposite directions, the carbonyl O sits exactly opposite the amide H so the hydrogen bonds are straight (near-linear), whereas when strands run in the same direction the carbonyl O is not directly opposite the amide H, so the hydrogen bonds are bent significantly. Because straighter hydrogen bonds are stronger, the opposite-direction arrangement is the more stable and higher-melting one, which is why short same-direction sheets are comparatively uncommon. (Choice A) is correct: domain X has the near-linear hydrogen bonds and higher melting temperature that are the signature of the more stable, opposite-direction (antiparallel) sheet, while the bent bonds and lower stability of domain Y match the same-direction (parallel) arrangement. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the relationship—it is the antiparallel (opposite-direction) sheet, not the parallel one, whose geometry allows the straight, stronger hydrogen bonds, so the labels are swapped. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(i \rightarrow i+4\) hydrogen bonds are the intra-strand bonds of an α-helix, not the inter-strand bonds of a β-sheet, so invoking them to explain a difference between two sheets misapplies helix geometry to sheet stability. (Choice D) is incorrect: the data attribute the stability difference to hydrogen-bond geometry (linear vs. bent) in otherwise matched domains, and nothing indicates cysteine content or disulfide cross-links, so adding disulfides is an unsupported explanation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the link between β-sheet hydrogen-bond geometry, strand orientation, and relative stability to interpret structural and thermal data and infer the strand arrangement.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c1c05fb2-4b38-4bce-b2e4-71df5b9c6308';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c1c05fb2-4b38-4bce-b2e4-71df5b9c6308';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c1c05fb2-4b38-4bce-b2e4-71df5b9c6308', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps which sheet orientation produces linear vs. bent H-bonds'),
  ('c1c05fb2-4b38-4bce-b2e4-71df5b9c6308', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses α-helix intra-strand i→i+4 H-bonds with β-sheet inter-strand H-bonds'),
  ('c1c05fb2-4b38-4bce-b2e4-71df5b9c6308', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'invokes disulfide cross-links not supported by the data to explain stability');

-- [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A protein biochemist characterizes the inter-strand hydrogen bonding in two different multi-strand \(\beta\)-sheet regions of a large protein. In region 1, the backbone hydrogen bonds are nearly linear: each carbonyl oxygen sits almost directly across from the amide hydrogen of the neighboring strand. In region 2, the corresponding hydrogen bonds are noticeably bent, so that each carbonyl oxygen is offset from — rather than aligned with — the amide hydrogen it bonds to. Based on this hydrogen-bond geometry alone, which conclusion is best supported?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The strands in region 1 run in opposite directions and, on average, form the more stable of the two sheet arrangements, whereas the strands in region 2 run in the same direction."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The strands in region 1 run in the same direction and form the less stable arrangement, whereas the strands in region 2 run in opposite directions and are more stable."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Region 2 cannot be a \\(\\beta\\)-sheet at all; bent backbone hydrogen bonds occur only within a single continuous strand, so region 2 must be an \\(\\alpha\\)-helix."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both regions have identically oriented strands; the difference in hydrogen-bond geometry reflects only the identity of the side chains and says nothing about strand direction."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' In a \(\beta\)-pleated sheet, extended strands are held together by inter-strand backbone hydrogen bonds, and the geometry of those bonds depends on whether neighboring strands run in opposite directions (antiparallel) or the same direction (parallel). The source states that in the antiparallel arrangement the carbonyl O on one strand sits "exactly opposite" the amide H on the adjacent strand, giving nearly linear hydrogen bonds, whereas in a parallel sheet the hydrogen bonds are "bent significantly" because the carbonyl O is not exactly opposite the amide H; because linear hydrogen bonds are stronger, antiparallel strands are "presumably more stable." (Choice A) is correct: the nearly linear, directly-across bonds of region 1 fingerprint an antiparallel sheet (strands running in opposite directions), which is the more stable arrangement, while the bent, offset bonds of region 2 fingerprint a parallel sheet (strands running the same direction). (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the assignment — linear/unbent hydrogen bonds correspond to the antiparallel (opposite-direction) sheet, not the parallel one, and the antiparallel arrangement is the more stable, so region 1 cannot be the less-stable same-direction sheet. (Choice C) is incorrect: bent inter-strand hydrogen bonds are exactly what a parallel \(\beta\)-sheet shows, so region 2 is still a sheet; the intra-strand (within a single continuous strand) hydrogen bonding that defines an \(\alpha\)-helix is a separate arrangement and is not implied by merely bent bonds between two side-by-side strands. (Choice D) is incorrect: the hydrogen-bond geometry is governed by backbone strand direction, not side-chain identity — side chains project perpendicular to the sheet and alternate above and below the plane, so they do not set whether the carbonyl O lies directly across from the amide H. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply how backbone hydrogen-bond geometry differs between antiparallel and parallel \(\beta\)-sheets to deduce strand orientation and relative stability from experimental observations.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd9ac37e5-bd0c-4972-843f-26c3c0bd06a5';
