-- [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studies a purified enzyme. Using a technique that measures the mass of the protein in its intact, folded state, she finds a single species of about 200 kDa. She then runs the same purified sample on SDS-PAGE — a gel that first heats the protein in an ionic detergent to unfold every chain — and observes four bands, each at about 50 kDa. No reducing agent was needed to produce the four bands. Which level of protein organization does the difference between these two results reveal about this enzyme?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Tertiary structure, because the detergent unfolded the 3-D shape of the intact chain into smaller fragments"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Quaternary structure, because the intact protein is an assembly of four subunits held together without covalent bonds"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Secondary structure, because the detergent disrupted the H-bonded helices and sheets that gave the protein its large size"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Primary structure, because the 50 kDa bands report the covalent amino-acid sequence that the detergent leaves intact"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The intact protein has a mass of ~200 kDa, but a denaturing gel that unfolds and separates individual polypeptide chains yields four equal ~50 kDa pieces (4 × 50 ≈ 200), and because no reducing agent was required, the pieces were not linked by disulfide bonds. The textbook notes that subunits "held together only by noncovalent interactions... will separate under denaturing conditions (SDS)" (LibreTexts 3.3.17, line 7505), so a large native mass that resolves into several smaller equal masses under SDS identifies a multi-subunit assembly. (Choice A) is incorrect: SDS does unfold tertiary structure, but unfolding a single chain does not cut it into four separate 50 kDa pieces — a single polypeptide would still migrate as one band at ~200 kDa, so the four-band result cannot reflect merely the 3-D folding of one chain. (Choice B) is correct: the drop from one 200 kDa native species to four equal 50 kDa chains, separable without breaking any covalent bonds, is the signature of quaternary structure — "the overall structure that arises when separate protein chains aggregate" and whose subunits "are held together by noncovalent interactions" (LibreTexts 4.3.4, lines 11244-11247). (Choice C) is incorrect: SDS also disrupts the H-bonds of secondary structure, but losing helices and sheets unfolds a chain without dividing it into multiple bands; secondary structure describes backbone H-bonding within a chain, not the number of chains present. (Choice D) is incorrect: SDS leaves peptide bonds intact, so each 50 kDa band does retain its primary sequence, but "primary structure is the linear sequence of the protein" (LibreTexts line 11243) of one chain and cannot explain why the native protein is four times as large — the relationship between the 200 kDa and 50 kDa results, not the sequence itself, is what the data reveal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the four structural levels to native- versus denaturing-mass data, inferring from a large native mass that resolves into equal noncovalently linked subunits that the protein possesses quaternary structure.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'cecdc170-c2c0-41fb-8be4-fc16da6d6d64';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'cecdc170-c2c0-41fb-8be4-fc16da6d6d64';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('cecdc170-c2c0-41fb-8be4-fc16da6d6d64', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses SDS unfolding of one chain''s tertiary structure with fragmentation into multiple subunits'),
  ('cecdc170-c2c0-41fb-8be4-fc16da6d6d64', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'correctly notes SDS breaks secondary-structure H-bonds but wrongly attributes the multi-band result to that'),
  ('cecdc170-c2c0-41fb-8be4-fc16da6d6d64', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'correctly notes SDS spares covalent sequence but uses that to name the wrong structural level');

-- [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'An enzyme in the liver catalyzes the following reaction:

CH₃CH₂OH + NAD⁺ → CH₃CHO + NADH + H⁺

Based on the chemistry of the reaction shown, into which Enzyme Commission (EC) class does this enzyme fall?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Transferase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Oxidoreductase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Isomerase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Lyase"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' EC classes are assigned by the type of reaction an enzyme catalyzes, so you must read the chemistry of the reaction rather than any enzyme name. In the reaction shown, ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH) loses two hydrogens to become acetaldehyde (CH₃CHO) — an oxidation — while the coenzyme NAD⁺ simultaneously gains a hydride to become NADH — a reduction. A reaction that couples an oxidation to a reduction is an oxidation–reduction (redox) reaction, which is exactly what oxidoreductases (EC1) catalyze. LibreTexts confirms this classification, stating that the first EC digit "stands for oxidoreductases" and that "''1.1.1.1'' is the EC number for alcohol dehydrogenase," the enzyme that "catalyzes the oxidation of ethanol, a primary alcohol, to acetaldehyde using an oxidizing agent called NAD+." (Choice A) is incorrect: transferases (EC2) move a discrete functional group between two molecules — for example a protein kinase that "covalently transfers a terminal phosphate (P) from ATP to a protein" — whereas here NAD⁺ is reduced rather than receiving an intact functional group, so no group transfer defines the reaction. (Choice B) is correct: the oxidation of ethanol to acetaldehyde coupled to the reduction of NAD⁺ to NADH is an electron-transfer (redox) reaction, the defining activity of oxidoreductases. (Choice C) is incorrect: isomerases (EC5), such as phosphoglucose isomerase converting "Glucose-6-phosphate → Fructose-6-phosphate," rearrange atoms within a single molecule with no change in oxidation state and no separate coenzyme being reduced, so the involvement of NAD⁺/NADH rules this class out. (Choice D) is incorrect: lyases (EC4), such as hydroxynitrile lyase, cleave a bond (or add a group to a double bond) without oxidation and without a redox coenzyme, so the reduction of NAD⁺ does not fit this class. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of each EC class to the chemistry shown, recognizing that the paired oxidation of ethanol and reduction of NAD⁺ identifies a redox reaction and therefore an oxidoreductase.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '37ddfbfc-2f91-4e88-b0fb-d6dacac853a7';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '37ddfbfc-2f91-4e88-b0fb-d6dacac853a7';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('37ddfbfc-2f91-4e88-b0fb-d6dacac853a7', 'A', 'misconception', 'Reads the hydride/H⁺ movement to NAD⁺ as a functional-group transfer and picks transferase'),
  ('37ddfbfc-2f91-4e88-b0fb-d6dacac853a7', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Sees two C₂ molecules of the same formula-class and treats ethanol→acetaldehyde as a simple rearrangement, ignoring the reduced NAD⁺'),
  ('37ddfbfc-2f91-4e88-b0fb-d6dacac853a7', 'D', 'misconception', 'Interprets loss of hydrogens / C–H bond change as a bond-cleaving lysis rather than an oxidation');

-- [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] ans=C hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A biochemist characterizes a small molecule that binds a metabolic enzyme at a site outside the active site. She measures the enzyme''s kinetics with and without a fixed concentration of the compound and obtains the following steady-state parameters:

| Condition | \(V_{max}\) (µmol/min) | \(K_m\) (mM) |
|---|---|---|
| No compound | 100 | 2.0 |
| + Compound | 60 | 3.5 |

On a Lineweaver-Burk plot, the two lines intersect to the left of the y-axis, above the x-axis. Which type of reversible inhibition is the compound most consistent with?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Competitive inhibition"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Uncompetitive inhibition"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Mixed inhibition"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Pure noncompetitive inhibition"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Reversible inhibitors are classified by which enzyme forms they bind and by their signature effects on \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\): competitive inhibitors bind only free E and raise apparent \(K_m\) while leaving \(V_{max}\) unchanged; uncompetitive inhibitors bind only ES and lower both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) proportionally (parallel Lineweaver-Burk lines); noncompetitive and mixed inhibitors bind both E and ES, with the source noting that in pure noncompetitive inhibition the dissociation constants of I for E and ES are equal so \(K_m\) is unchanged, whereas ''in the more general case, the Kd''s are different, and the inhibition is called mixed,'' in which ''both parameters change.'' Here \(V_{max}\) falls (100 → 60) and \(K_m\) does not stay fixed but rises (2.0 → 3.5 mM), and the Lineweaver-Burk lines intersect off the y-axis in the second quadrant — the pattern of a mixed inhibitor whose affinity for E exceeds its affinity for ES. (Choice A) is incorrect: a competitive inhibitor competes at the active site, raises apparent \(K_m\), but leaves \(V_{max}\) unchanged, so the drop from 100 to 60 rules it out (and the compound binds away from the active site). (Choice B) is incorrect: an uncompetitive inhibitor binds only ES and lowers both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\), giving parallel lines with no intersection; here \(K_m\) rises rather than falls, and the lines intersect. (Choice C) is correct: both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) change and the lines intersect to the left of the y-axis, the hallmark of a mixed inhibitor that binds both E and ES with unequal affinities. (Choice D) is incorrect: in pure noncompetitive inhibition the inhibitor binds E and ES with equal affinity, so \(V_{max}\) falls but apparent \(K_m\) is unchanged and the Lineweaver-Burk lines intersect exactly on the x-axis; the observed shift in \(K_m\) from 2.0 to 3.5 mM excludes it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic effects of each inhibition type on \(V_{max}\), \(K_m\), and the Lineweaver-Burk intersection to interpret the data and distinguish mixed from noncompetitive inhibition.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '34374aed-564f-451e-bd4c-c7ad5c57b6fd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '34374aed-564f-451e-bd4c-c7ad5c57b6fd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('34374aed-564f-451e-bd4c-c7ad5c57b6fd', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses raised Km with competitive inhibition while ignoring the Vmax decrease'),
  ('34374aed-564f-451e-bd4c-c7ad5c57b6fd', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'knows uncompetitive changes both parameters but forgets Km must fall and lines are parallel'),
  ('34374aed-564f-451e-bd4c-c7ad5c57b6fd', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes any inhibitor binding outside the active site that lowers Vmax is noncompetitive, overlooking that Km must stay unchanged');

-- [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Hexokinase catalyzes the transfer of a phosphoryl group from ATP to glucose. X-ray studies show that the enzyme''s two lobes stay apart when the enzyme sits empty in solution or holds only ATP, but swing tightly shut around glucose once glucose enters the cleft, and the phosphoryl-transfer chemistry proceeds efficiently only in this closed state. A biochemist proposes that this behavior is what allows hexokinase to react with glucose while largely ignoring other small molecules that could drift into the open cleft. Which principle, if correct, best supports the biochemist''s proposal?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Because the catalytically active arrangement forms only when the correct substrate itself drives the lobes shut, molecules that fail to trigger this closure are never brought into a reactive geometry"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Because a bound divalent metal ion such as Mg2+ neutralizes ATP''s phosphate charges, only molecules able to coordinate that metal can be turned over"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Because the closed lobes lower the activation energy for any molecule they happen to enclose, catalysis proceeds fastest for whatever occupies the cleft"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Because water is excluded from the closed cleft by hydrophobic packing, the enzyme''s shape stays fixed and identical whether or not glucose is present"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The behavior described is the induced-fit model of enzyme-substrate binding: contact with the correct substrate changes the enzyme''s shape so the active site molds around that substrate and the reaction can proceed. LibreTexts describes the induced-fit model as one in which "contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein''s shape, allowing it to bind" and states that "substrate interaction causes the enzyme''s shape to change to better fit the substrate and mediate the chemical reaction" (LibreTexts 732, 739-740), unlike the lock-and-key model in which substrates bind "without further modification to the enzyme''s shape" (LibreTexts 738-739). The key insight is that hexokinase''s catalytic machinery is assembled only by the conformational change, and only the genuine substrate can trigger that change. (Choice A) is correct: because the closed, catalytically competent geometry is produced only when the correct substrate itself drives the lobes shut, a molecule that cannot induce closure is never positioned for phosphoryl transfer, so making catalysis contingent on substrate-triggered closure is what confers the observed selectivity. (Choice B) is incorrect: Mg2+ genuinely coordinates and neutralizes ATP''s negatively charged phosphates, but that cofactor chemistry does not depend on the lobe-closing motion and does not explain why non-substrate molecules are ignored; the metal requirement would apply whether the enzyme were rigid or flexible. (Choice C) is incorrect: it overgeneralizes transition-state stabilization by claiming the closed cleft speeds the reaction for "any molecule they happen to enclose" — that would predict wasteful reaction with whatever is bound and cannot account for selectivity, since specificity here comes from which molecule can trigger closure, not from indiscriminate rate enhancement once closed. (Choice D) is incorrect: while it is true that solvent is excluded from the closed cleft, its claim that the enzyme''s shape "stays fixed and identical whether or not glucose is present" directly contradicts the X-ray observation that the lobes swing shut around glucose; it describes a rigid lock-and-key enzyme and denies the conformational change the scenario reports. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the induced-fit model to evaluate a proposed mechanistic explanation and identify the principle that actually produces the enzyme''s selectivity.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0b0f8f8b-e512-4206-8e0f-0827821b5291';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0b0f8f8b-e512-4206-8e0f-0827821b5291';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0b0f8f8b-e512-4206-8e0f-0827821b5291', 'B', 'partial_truth', 'real cofactor fact (Mg2+ coordinates ATP) that is independent of the conformational motion'),
  ('0b0f8f8b-e512-4206-8e0f-0827821b5291', 'C', 'misconception', 'transition-state/activation-energy lowering misattributed as indiscriminate, ignoring what triggers closure'),
  ('0b0f8f8b-e512-4206-8e0f-0827821b5291', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'lock-and-key ''fixed shape'' claim that contradicts the reported induced conformational change, baited with a true water-exclusion clause');

-- [Irreversible Inhibition] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studies how iodoacetamide, an inhibitor that abolishes activity of a particular enzyme, exerts its effect. Two identical enzyme samples are treated with the same amount of iodoacetamide. Sample 1 is treated directly. Sample 2 is first saturated with a high concentration of a substrate analog that binds reversibly to the active site, and only then is iodoacetamide added; the analog is later washed away. After extensive dialysis of both samples to remove all small molecules, Sample 1 shows almost no activity while Sample 2 recovers most of its original activity. Which conclusion is best supported by these results?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The iodoacetamide acts on a residue in the active site, and pre-binding the analog shielded that residue from covalent modification."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The substrate analog competes with iodoacetamide for a reversible allosteric site, and dialysis removed both from Sample 2."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The substrate analog raised the enzyme''s affinity for substrate, so Sample 2 simply outcompeted the inhibitor for available substrate."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The iodoacetamide denatured the enzyme in Sample 1, and the analog in Sample 2 stabilized the fold so it could refold after dialysis."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Irreversible inhibitors such as iodoacetamide work by covalently modifying an essential side chain (e.g., a cysteine) in or near the active site, so activity is not restored by dialysis or dilution. A classic way to identify and protect that essential residue is to first occupy the active site with a saturating concentration of a reversibly binding ligand: the bound ligand physically blocks the reactive residue, so the covalent inhibitor cannot reach it, and once the protecting ligand is washed away the enzyme is active. The LibreTexts text states that irreversible inhibitors ''modify key amino acid side chains (e.g., cysteine modification by iodoacetamide) to permanently inactivate an enzyme'' and describes protecting the essential residue by first saturating ''the active site with... a ligand that binds reversibly at the active site,'' after which ''the critical side chain would be protected from chemical modification.'' (Choice A) is correct: the results show the analog protected the enzyme only because iodoacetamide targets an essential active-site residue—reversible occupancy of that site blocked the covalent reaction, and the covalent nature of the inhibition is why dialysis alone did not rescue Sample 1. (Choice B) is incorrect: a substrate analog binds the active site, not a separate allosteric site, and if the inhibition were merely reversible binding at an allosteric site, dialysis would have restored Sample 1 as well—yet Sample 1 stayed inactive, which is the hallmark of covalent (irreversible) modification. (Choice C) is incorrect: no substrate is present during dialysis and the analog is washed away, so ''outcompeting for substrate'' cannot explain the recovery; competition for substrate describes competitive reversible inhibition, not protection against a covalent reagent. (Choice D) is incorrect: iodoacetamide inactivates by covalently alkylating a specific residue, not by denaturing the fold, and a small alkylating reagent occupying one side chain would not be reversed by refolding after dialysis. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of irreversible covalent inhibition to interpret a protection experiment and infer that the inhibitor targets an essential active-site residue.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '08698680-01f7-4344-ab72-28cca2031476';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '08698680-01f7-4344-ab72-28cca2031476';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('08698680-01f7-4344-ab72-28cca2031476', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses covalent irreversible inhibition with reversible allosteric binding'),
  ('08698680-01f7-4344-ab72-28cca2031476', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'imports competitive-inhibition ''outcompeting substrate'' logic into a covalent-protection context'),
  ('08698680-01f7-4344-ab72-28cca2031476', 'D', 'misconception', 'conflates covalent active-site modification with global denaturation/refolding');

-- [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A biochemist characterizes a purified enzyme by measuring initial velocity across a range of substrate concentrations, generating a full saturation curve. She then repeats the entire experiment after doubling the total amount of enzyme in the assay, keeping temperature, pH, and the range of \([S]\) identical. Compared with the first experiment, how should the plateau (limiting) initial velocity approached at very high \([S]\), and the substrate concentration required to reach half of that plateau, change in the second experiment?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The plateau velocity doubles, while the substrate concentration at half-plateau is essentially unchanged"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Both the plateau velocity and the substrate concentration at half-plateau double"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The plateau velocity is unchanged, while the substrate concentration at half-plateau is halved"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The plateau velocity is unchanged, while the substrate concentration at half-plateau doubles"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The plateau velocity approached at saturating substrate is \(V_{max}\), and the substrate concentration giving half of that plateau is \(K_m\). The text states that for all reactions \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\) (line 27225), so \(V_{max}\) is directly proportional to total enzyme concentration; \(K_m\) and the turnover number \(k_{cat}\) are intrinsic properties of the enzyme–substrate pair and do not depend on how much enzyme is present. (Choice A) is correct: because \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), doubling the total enzyme doubles the plateau velocity; \(K_m\) (units of molarity, the substrate concentration at which velocity is half of \(V_{max}\), line 27363) is a per-molecule property independent of \([E]_0\), so the half-plateau \([S]\) is essentially unchanged. (Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly scales \(V_{max}\) with enzyme amount but wrongly treats \(K_m\) as if it also scaled with enzyme concentration; \(K_m\) is set by the rate constants of the enzyme–substrate interaction, not by \([E]_0\). (Choice C) is incorrect: it holds \(V_{max}\) constant, contradicting \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), and invents a decrease in \(K_m\) as if more enzyme raised affinity, which it does not. (Choice D) is incorrect: it also wrongly holds \(V_{max}\) constant and, in addition, treats added enzyme as if it weakened binding (raising \(K_m\)); neither \(V_{max}\) staying fixed nor \(K_m\) changing follows from adding enzyme. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\) and the definition of \(K_m\) to predict how each parameter responds to a change in enzyme concentration.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'da94beb0-8f76-4672-86d1-bcfc9ebc411c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'da94beb0-8f76-4672-86d1-bcfc9ebc411c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('da94beb0-8f76-4672-86d1-bcfc9ebc411c', 'B', 'misconception', 'Assumes every parameter scales with enzyme amount'),
  ('da94beb0-8f76-4672-86d1-bcfc9ebc411c', 'C', 'misconception', 'Believes Vmax is a fixed enzyme property and more enzyme increases affinity'),
  ('da94beb0-8f76-4672-86d1-bcfc9ebc411c', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'Treats added enzyme as changing Km (binding) rather than Vmax');

-- [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two engineered variants of the same enzyme are assayed under identical conditions and at identical enzyme concentrations. Both variants reach the same maximal initial velocity, \(V_{max}\). However, Variant 1 reaches one-half of \(V_{max}\) at a substrate concentration of 0.2 mM, whereas Variant 2 requires a substrate concentration of 2.0 mM to reach one-half of \(V_{max}\). Which conclusion is best supported by these data?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Variant 1 has a higher apparent affinity for the substrate than Variant 2"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Variant 1 has a higher turnover number, \\(k_{cat}\\), than Variant 2"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Variant 2 has a higher maximal velocity, \\(V_{max}\\), than Variant 1"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Variant 2 has a higher apparent affinity for the substrate than Variant 1"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The substrate concentration at which an enzyme reaches half of \(V_{max}\) is, by definition, its Michaelis constant \(K_m\), which the textbook says "is operationally defined as the substrate concentration at which the initial velocity is half of VM" (lines 27363-27364) and which can be thought of as an "effective" dissociation constant for the enzyme-substrate complex (line 27364). A smaller \(K_m\) therefore means the enzyme reaches half-maximal velocity at a lower substrate concentration, reflecting a higher apparent affinity — the same inverse logic seen for dissociation constants, where "lower KD values indicate tighter binding" (line 19550). Here Variant 1 has \(K_m = 0.2\) mM and Variant 2 has \(K_m = 2.0\) mM. (Choice A) is correct: Variant 1 reaches half-\(V_{max}\) at a tenfold lower substrate concentration, so it has the smaller \(K_m\) and thus the higher apparent affinity for the substrate. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(k_{cat}\) is the turnover number in units of s\(^{-1}\) (line 27366), and because \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), equal \(V_{max}\) at equal enzyme concentration means the two variants have equal \(k_{cat}\), not a higher one for Variant 1. (Choice C) is incorrect: the stem states that both variants reach the same \(V_{max}\); the difference lies entirely in the substrate concentration needed to reach half of that shared plateau, not in the plateau itself. (Choice D) is incorrect: this reverses the relationship — Variant 2 needs ten times more substrate to reach half-\(V_{max}\), so it has the larger \(K_m\) and the lower apparent affinity, not the higher one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the meaning of the half-\(V_{max}\) substrate concentration (\(K_m\)) to interpret comparative kinetic data and infer relative substrate affinity, recognizing that a lower \(K_m\) reflects tighter binding.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a4b8b72a-dbf8-4bc7-ab31-289413f3d86c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a4b8b72a-dbf8-4bc7-ab31-289413f3d86c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a4b8b72a-dbf8-4bc7-ab31-289413f3d86c', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing Km with catalytic rate (kcat), treating the half-max substrate concentration as a measure of catalytic speed rather than binding'),
  ('a4b8b72a-dbf8-4bc7-ab31-289413f3d86c', 'C', 'misconception', 'confusing Km with Vmax, ignoring the stem''s statement that Vmax is equal and misreading the half-max point as a difference in maximal velocity'),
  ('a4b8b72a-dbf8-4bc7-ab31-289413f3d86c', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'inverting the Km-affinity relationship, concluding that the higher Km (more substrate needed) means higher affinity');

-- [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two enzymes, X and Y, act on the same class of small-molecule substrates. Enzyme X binds its substrate strictly according to a lock-and-key mechanism, whereas enzyme Y binds by an induced-fit mechanism. A biochemist screens both enzymes against a panel of close structural analogs of the natural substrate. Which result is most consistent with the difference between the two binding mechanisms?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Enzyme Y accepts a broader range of the analogs than enzyme X, because its active site can adjust its shape upon binding"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Enzyme X accepts a broader range of the analogs than enzyme Y, because its preformed rigid pocket presents a ready-made site that many analogs can slot into"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both enzymes accept a similar range of the analogs, because the two mechanisms affect how quickly binding occurs rather than which structures are accepted"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neither enzyme accepts the analogs, because both models require the substrate to match the natural one closely enough that structural variants are excluded"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The two binding models differ in whether the enzyme''s shape changes on binding: in the lock-and-key model "substrates bind to the enzyme''s active site without further modification to the enzyme''s shape," so the reactant must fit "the exact shape of the enzyme''s binding site," whereas in the induced-fit model "contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein''s shape, allowing it to bind" (LibreTexts 731-732, 738-739). Because a lock-and-key site is preformed and rigid, it is highly specific and tolerates little deviation from the natural substrate, while an induced-fit site can remold itself around a binding partner and therefore accommodate a wider set of related structures. (Choice A) is correct: an induced-fit active site adjusts its geometry when a ligand contacts it, so enzyme Y can close around analogs that do not exactly match the free-enzyme shape, giving it greater tolerance for the panel than the rigid lock-and-key enzyme X. (Choice B) is incorrect: the lock-and-key site is rigid and preformed, which makes it more restrictive, not less; a fixed pocket demands close complementarity rather than accepting many shapes, so the reasoning inverts the mechanism. (Choice C) is incorrect: the models differ specifically in enzyme flexibility and thus in how strictly the site discriminates among structures, so they need not accept a similar range — the distinction is about conformational change, not merely how fast binding occurs. (Choice D) is incorrect: neither model demands a perfect match with the natural substrate for every analog; lock-and-key requires close complementarity but still binds sufficiently similar structures, and induced fit is even more permissive, so predicting zero binding for both contradicts both mechanisms. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining criterion of each binding model — whether the active site changes shape on binding — to predict how substrate tolerance differs between a rigid and a flexible enzyme.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c8f6b0d4-23af-4a8e-a01d-f6c9ed2e25c5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c8f6b0d4-23af-4a8e-a01d-f6c9ed2e25c5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c8f6b0d4-23af-4a8e-a01d-f6c9ed2e25c5', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps which model is more permissive'),
  ('c8f6b0d4-23af-4a8e-a01d-f6c9ed2e25c5', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes the models differ only in kinetics, not specificity'),
  ('c8f6b0d4-23af-4a8e-a01d-f6c9ed2e25c5', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats both models as demanding a perfect exact match');

-- [Lineweaver-Burk (Double-Reciprocal) Plots] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A biochemist measures the initial velocity of an enzyme at several substrate concentrations and plots her data as \(1/v_0\) versus \(1/[S]\). The data fall on a straight line described by \(1/v_0 = 0.20\,(1/[S]) + 0.010\), where \(v_0\) is in µmol·min\(^{-1}\) and \([S]\) is in µM. Based on this fitted line, what are the enzyme''s \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(V_{max} = 100\\) µmol·min\\(^{-1}\\), \\(K_m = 20\\) µM"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(V_{max} = 0.010\\) µmol·min\\(^{-1}\\), \\(K_m = 0.20\\) µM"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(V_{max} = 100\\) µmol·min\\(^{-1}\\), \\(K_m = 5\\) µM"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(V_{max} = 5\\) µmol·min\\(^{-1}\\), \\(K_m = 100\\) µM"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' A Lineweaver-Burk (double-reciprocal) plot linearizes the Michaelis-Menten equation so that \(1/v_0 = (K_m/V_{max})(1/[S]) + 1/V_{max}\); the reference states that ''plots of 1/v vs 1/S are straight lines with slope m = KM/VM, and y-intercept b = 1/VM'' (line 27475), and the x-intercept equals \(-1/K_m\) (line 26959). To recover the kinetic constants you must invert and combine the line''s parameters, not read them off directly. Here the y-intercept is \(0.010\) and the slope is \(0.20\). (Choice A) is correct: the y-intercept gives \(1/V_{max} = 0.010\), so \(V_{max} = 1/0.010 = 100\) µmol·min\(^{-1}\); the slope gives \(K_m/V_{max} = 0.20\), so \(K_m = 0.20 \times V_{max} = 0.20 \times 100 = 20\) µM (equivalently, \(K_m = -1/\text{x-intercept}\), and the x-intercept is \(-0.010/0.20 = -0.050\), giving \(K_m = 20\) µM). (Choice B) is incorrect: it takes the y-intercept (\(0.010\)) and slope (\(0.20\)) as the parameters themselves, forgetting that on a double-reciprocal plot both must be inverted — the intercept is \(1/V_{max}\), not \(V_{max}\). (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly inverts the y-intercept for \(V_{max}\) but then computes \(K_m = 1/\text{slope} = 1/0.20 = 5\), mistakenly treating the slope as if it were \(1/K_m\); the slope is actually \(K_m/V_{max}\). (Choice D) is incorrect: it swaps the roles of the slope and y-intercept, taking \(V_{max} = 1/\text{slope} = 5\) and \(K_m = 1/\text{y-intercept} = 100\); the y-intercept encodes \(V_{max}\) and the slope encodes \(K_m/V_{max}\), not the reverse. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Lineweaver-Burk relationships (slope \(= K_m/V_{max}\), y-intercept \(= 1/V_{max}\)) to a fitted line to solve for both kinetic constants.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2593613b-cc82-4cb0-874e-8cb92df3b9c4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2593613b-cc82-4cb0-874e-8cb92df3b9c4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2593613b-cc82-4cb0-874e-8cb92df3b9c4', 'B', 'misconception', 'reads plot parameters directly without inverting'),
  ('2593613b-cc82-4cb0-874e-8cb92df3b9c4', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats slope as 1/Km instead of Km/Vmax'),
  ('2593613b-cc82-4cb0-874e-8cb92df3b9c4', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps roles of slope and y-intercept');

-- [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two homologous enzymes catalyze the same reaction: one from a mesophilic bacterium and one from a thermophilic bacterium that grows near hydrothermal vents. Each is assayed for catalytic rate across 0–90 °C, and separately its fraction of unfolded protein is measured by a spectroscopic melting assay. The results are tabulated below.

| Enzyme | Catalytic rate peaks at | 50% of protein unfolded at | Rate falls to ~0 by |
|--------|-------------------------|----------------------------|---------------------|
| Mesophilic | 40 °C | 46 °C | 55 °C |
| Thermophilic | 75 °C | 82 °C | 88 °C |

For the thermophilic enzyme, the researcher wants to report the single parameter that a physician-scientist could use to predict the temperature at which this enzyme would work fastest inside its host organism. Which value should be reported, and by what term?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "75 °C, the enzyme''s optimal temperature"}, {"label": "B", "text": "88 °C, the enzyme''s denaturation temperature"}, {"label": "C", "text": "82 °C, the enzyme''s melting temperature"}, {"label": "D", "text": "46 °C, the enzyme''s optimal temperature"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Enzyme catalytic rate rises with temperature up to a peak and then falls sharply as heat unfolds (denatures) the protein; LibreTexts notes that from roughly 0 to 40–50 °C enzyme activity usually increases as do most reaction rates, and that at higher temperatures activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures. The temperature at which activity is maximal is the enzyme''s optimal temperature, and thermophilic enzymes are shifted to higher optima than their mesophilic homologs. (Choice A) is correct: the value that predicts where the enzyme works fastest is the peak of the rate-versus-temperature curve, which for the thermophilic enzyme is 75 °C, and that peak is by definition the optimal temperature. (Choice B) is incorrect: 88 °C is where the thermophilic enzyme''s rate has fallen to nearly zero because the protein is essentially fully denatured; this denaturation endpoint marks the loss of activity, not the temperature of fastest catalysis. (Choice C) is incorrect: 82 °C is the melting temperature (Tm), the point at which 50% of the protein population is unfolded — a stability parameter describing the unfolding transition, which lies above and is distinct from the activity peak. (Choice D) is incorrect: 46 °C is the mesophilic homolog''s Tm, not any value from the thermophilic enzyme, and it is mislabeled as an optimal temperature; picking it confuses the two enzymes and confuses unfolding with peak activity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between temperature, catalytic rate, and protein unfolding to select the correct value from data and match it to the term optimal temperature, distinguishing it from denaturation and melting temperatures.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ceaa5293-654d-48b7-9c77-08d2eaf87379';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ceaa5293-654d-48b7-9c77-08d2eaf87379';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ceaa5293-654d-48b7-9c77-08d2eaf87379', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses activity-loss endpoint with peak activity'),
  ('ceaa5293-654d-48b7-9c77-08d2eaf87379', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'reports a real stability parameter (Tm) instead of the activity optimum'),
  ('ceaa5293-654d-48b7-9c77-08d2eaf87379', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'pulls a real value from the wrong enzyme and mislabels it');

-- [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher purifies two enzymes and measures each one''s initial velocity across a range of buffer pH values, holding temperature, ionic strength, and substrate concentration constant. Enzyme X (a gastric protease) shows peak activity near pH 2, whereas Enzyme Y peaks near pH 8; each profile has a single maximum flanked by declining activity on both sides. Which of the following best explains why the two enzymes reach maximal activity at such different pH values?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Each enzyme''s active site contains ionizable catalytic side chains that must be in a particular protonation state, and those states are reached at different pH values for the two enzymes"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Enzyme X operates at higher substrate concentrations than Enzyme Y, and greater saturation of the active site shifts the velocity peak to a lower pH"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both enzymes are actually most active at pH 7, and the apparent peaks at pH 2 and pH 8 are artifacts of irreversible denaturation at the tested extremes"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Enzyme X contains more disulfide bonds than Enzyme Y, giving it the greater conformational stability needed to function at a lower pH"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The velocity-versus-pH profile of an enzyme is bell-shaped because catalysis depends on specific ionizable side chains in the active site being in the correct protonation (charge) state; the text notes that a side chain ''must be deprotonated for optimal activity of trypsin, which shows increases in activity with pH centered at pH 6'' (line 28512) and that ''a deprotonated histidine is necessary for optimal catalysis'' (line 30634). Because different enzymes rely on different ionizable residues with different pKa values, each has its own characteristic optimal pH rather than a universal one, so a gastric protease can peak near pH 2 while another enzyme peaks near pH 8. (Choice A) is correct: the optimum reflects the pH at which the enzyme''s catalytic residues carry the charges needed for substrate binding and turnover, and because those residues (and their pKa values) differ between enzymes, the two optima fall at different pH values. (Choice B) is incorrect: substrate concentration is held constant in the experiment, and even if it varied, saturation raises the plateau velocity (relates to \(V_{max}\)) without relocating the pH optimum, which is set by protonation states, not by \([S]\). (Choice C) is incorrect: the text states ''pH has a marked effect on the velocity of enzyme-catalyzed reactions'' (line 28499) and optima are enzyme-specific rather than uniformly neutral; while extreme pH can cause irreversible denaturation, that would abolish the peak, not manufacture a reproducible single maximum at pH 2 or pH 8. (Choice D) is incorrect: disulfide bonds contribute to conformational stability, but stability does not determine the pH at which the reaction rate is maximal—the position of the optimum is governed by the protonation state of catalytic residues, so a more stable enzyme would not automatically be optimal at low pH. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that an enzyme''s pH optimum reflects the protonation states of its ionizable catalytic side chains to explain why two enzymes with different active-site residues peak at different pH values.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '7d6e217d-1546-4388-a079-6efc22efe02e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7d6e217d-1546-4388-a079-6efc22efe02e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7d6e217d-1546-4388-a079-6efc22efe02e', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflating pH optimum with substrate saturation/[S] effects'),
  ('7d6e217d-1546-4388-a079-6efc22efe02e', 'C', 'misconception', 'belief that all enzymes are optimal at neutral pH 7'),
  ('7d6e217d-1546-4388-a079-6efc22efe02e', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'invoking conformational stability (disulfides) as the cause of the pH optimum');

-- [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A five-step biosynthetic pathway converts substrate S1 into end product P5. A researcher purifies enzyme E1, which catalyzes the first committed step (S1 → S2), and measures its activity after separately adding several compounds at physiological concentrations. The results are:

| Compound added        | E1 activity (% of control) |
|-----------------------|----------------------------|
| None (control)        | 100                        |
| Extra S1 (substrate)  | 118                        |
| S3 (an intermediate)  | 96                         |
| P5 (end product)      | 22                         |
| Q (product of a separate pathway) | 101            |

The cell relies on this pattern of regulation to keep flux through the pathway matched to its need for P5. Which prediction about the pathway''s behavior is best supported by these data?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "When P5 rises well above the cell''s needs, E1 slows and S1 stops being drawn into the pathway, conserving S1 until P5 is consumed."}, {"label": "B", "text": "When S1 rises, E1 accelerates so strongly that P5 accumulates faster than any downstream enzyme can regulate."}, {"label": "C", "text": "When S3 accumulates, it feeds back on E1 to halt the pathway before the final product can form."}, {"label": "D", "text": "When Q from the separate pathway rises, it shuts E1 down to coordinate the two pathways."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' In allosteric feedback regulation, the end product of a pathway binds an enzyme catalyzing an earlier (often the first, committed) step and inhibits it, so accumulation of the product throttles its own further synthesis; the textbook describes ''feedback inhibition from downstream products'' as a common allosteric mechanism (line 31126) and gives serine binding an allosteric site on PGDH to inhibit ''the first reaction of a pathway'' (lines 31143-31144) and CTP/UTP feeding back to inhibit aspartate transcarbamylase (line 21091). The data must drive the answer: only the end product P5 sharply lowers E1 activity (22% of control), while substrate, an intermediate, and an unrelated product do not, so the student must infer the regulatory consequence rather than recognize a named term. (Choice A) is correct: P5 (and only P5) strongly inhibits E1, the first committed enzyme, so as P5 exceeds demand it suppresses entry of S1 into the pathway and production self-limits until P5 falls again — exactly the negative feedback the data show and the logic that matches serine→PGDH and CTP/UTP→ATCase. (Choice B) is incorrect: extra substrate raises E1 activity only modestly (118%), and describing this as runaway, unregulated acceleration ignores that the same data show a powerful brake (P5 at 22%); the text notes upstream substrates can drive feedforward activation (line 31126), but that is a small effect here and is not what keeps flux matched to need. (Choice C) is incorrect: the intermediate S3 leaves E1 essentially unchanged (96%), so the data give no support for an intermediate acting as the feedback signal; genuine end-product feedback comes from the pathway''s final product, not a mid-pathway metabolite. (Choice D) is incorrect: Q, a product of a separate pathway, has no effect on E1 (101%), so the data do not support cross-pathway shutdown; feedback inhibition here uses the pathway''s own end product acting on its own early enzyme, as in the serine and CTP/UTP examples. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the logic of end-product feedback inhibition to activity data and predict how the pathway''s flux responds, rather than recall a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a66ea58a-c2b8-4b94-a445-0c03348c7023';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a66ea58a-c2b8-4b94-a445-0c03348c7023';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a66ea58a-c2b8-4b94-a445-0c03348c7023', 'B', 'misconception', 'treats modest feedforward-type activation by substrate as unregulated runaway acceleration, ignoring the strong end-product brake in the data'),
  ('a66ea58a-c2b8-4b94-a445-0c03348c7023', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes a mid-pathway intermediate rather than the true end product is the feedback signal, contradicted by S3 having no effect'),
  ('a66ea58a-c2b8-4b94-a445-0c03348c7023', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'invokes real cross-pathway coordination but attributes it to a compound (Q) the data show has no effect on E1');

-- [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two already-active enzymes are prepared in vitro. Enzyme 1 is a digestive protease that was generated from its secreted precursor after an upstream protease removed an N-terminal peptide fragment. Enzyme 2 is a cytoplasmic enzyme that was switched on when a kinase transferred a phosphate from ATP onto one of its serine residues. A researcher now wants to return each enzyme to its inactive state and adds, to both samples, only the appropriate "off" catalyst for each system (a phosphatase for Enzyme 2, and a fresh supply of the same upstream protease for Enzyme 1). Assuming no protein synthesis machinery is present, which prediction is best supported?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Only Enzyme 2 can be returned to its inactive form, because the phosphate on its serine can be hydrolyzed off, while the peptide bond that was cut cannot be re-formed to restore Enzyme 1''s precursor"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Only Enzyme 1 can be returned to its inactive form, because adding more of the upstream protease will reattach the removed N-terminal fragment"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both enzymes can be returned to their inactive forms, because in each case the catalyst that turned the enzyme on can also reverse the same modification"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neither enzyme can be returned to its inactive form, because both modifications are covalent and covalent changes to a protein cannot be undone once made"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Zymogen activation works by proteolytic cleavage — the enzyme ''loses the first 15 amino acids by selective proteolysis'' (line 30094-30095), a hydrolysis that severs a peptide bond and discards a fragment, and the cell has no machinery here to re-form that bond, so it cannot be undone (lines 31299-31300). Phosphorylation is a covalent modification installed by ''kinases'' and removed by ''phosphatases'' (line 6247-6250), so a phosphatase can hydrolyze the phosphate ester off the serine and reset that enzyme. You must reason from the underlying chemistry: cutting out a piece of the polypeptide is a one-way change, whereas adding then removing a phosphate group is a reversible cycle. (Choice A) is correct: the phosphatase hydrolyzes the serine phosphate off Enzyme 2 and returns it to its inactive dephosphorylated state, but no cellular process can rejoin the cleaved peptide bond or replace the excised N-terminal fragment of Enzyme 1, so Enzyme 1 stays active (lines 30094-30095, 6247-6250). (Choice B) is incorrect: adding more upstream protease cannot ligate the fragment back on — a protease hydrolyzes peptide bonds, it does not form them, so more protease would at most cleave further, never restore the precursor (line 30094-30095). (Choice C) is incorrect: it wrongly treats both ''off'' catalysts as reversing the activating step; the phosphatase does reverse phosphorylation, but the upstream protease does not reverse cleavage because bond hydrolysis discards a fragment that cannot be re-added (lines 31299-31300, 6247-6250). (Choice D) is incorrect: ''covalent'' does not mean ''permanent'' — the phosphoester bond is a covalent modification that a phosphatase readily removes, so Enzyme 2 can indeed be inactivated (line 6247-6250). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between bond-cleaving activation and group-transfer activation to predict which chemical change a cellular catalyst can undo and which it cannot.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd86cd542-b722-433a-8c86-590d9ac45b57';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd86cd542-b722-433a-8c86-590d9ac45b57';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d86cd542-b722-433a-8c86-590d9ac45b57', 'B', 'misconception', 'believes a protease can re-ligate the fragment it removed / that adding the activating enzyme reverses the change'),
  ('d86cd542-b722-433a-8c86-590d9ac45b57', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes any ''off'' catalyst symmetrically reverses its own activating step for both systems'),
  ('d86cd542-b722-433a-8c86-590d9ac45b57', 'D', 'misconception', 'conflates ''covalent modification'' with ''permanent/irreversible,'' overlooking that phosphatases reverse phosphorylation');

-- [CNS Tissue Organization] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a brain-slice experiment, a researcher can selectively silence one CNS glial cell type at a time while stimulating a nearby cluster of neurons to fire in rapid, sustained bursts. When one particular glial type is silenced, the neurons fire normally at first but progressively lose the ability to return to their resting membrane potential between action potentials, and after several seconds they begin firing erratically and eventually fail — even though their myelination, energy supply, and physical structure remain intact. Silencing of which glial cell type best accounts for this result?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The astrocyte"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The oligodendrocyte"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The microglial cell"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The ependymal cell"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' The resting membrane potential and its recovery after each action potential depend on the concentrations of ions—especially K+—in the extracellular fluid around a neuron, and each of the four CNS glial cell types performs a distinct supporting job. OpenStax A&P 2e notes that astrocytes support CNS neurons by "maintaining the concentration of chemicals in the extracellular space," and that during rapid, sustained firing (as in an ischemic event where "extracellular K+ levels are elevated") the "astrocytes in the area are equipped to clear excess K+ to aid the pump"; when astrocytes fail to buffer these ions, neurons cannot re-establish their resting potential and signaling collapses. The scenario describes exactly that failure mode—normal myelination, energy, and structure but a progressive inability to reset the membrane potential during heavy activity—so the student must reason backward from the symptom to the cell responsible. (Choice A) is correct: astrocytes maintain the extracellular ionic environment, clearing the K+ that accumulates during repeated firing; without them, K+ builds up outside the neurons, the resting potential cannot be restored, and firing becomes erratic and then fails—matching the observed result. (Choice B) is incorrect: oligodendrocytes insulate axons with myelin to speed conduction, but the scenario states myelination is intact, and loss of myelin would slow or block propagation rather than cause a progressive failure to reset the resting potential during sustained firing. (Choice C) is incorrect: microglia are the resident macrophages that phagocytose damaged cells and pathogens; losing their immune surveillance would not, on the timescale of seconds of stimulation, prevent healthy neurons from restoring their membrane potential. (Choice D) is incorrect: ependymal cells filter blood at the choroid plexus to produce cerebrospinal fluid, a bulk-fluid function; silencing them does not locally control the ion concentrations immediately surrounding actively firing neurons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct functional roles of the CNS glial cell types to infer which cell''s loss would specifically prevent neurons from restoring their resting membrane potential during sustained activity.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'Confusing myelin insulation with ionic maintenance'),
  ('43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Attributing the effect to immune/macrophage function'),
  ('43111300-983f-46bf-b4bd-1aaa25037bd9', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'Confusing bulk CSF production with local ion regulation');

COMMIT;
